const LESSONS = [{"id": "1", "title": "Variables", "html": "<p><strong>Names refer to objects. Assignment binds a name; it does not automatically copy an object.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>count = 3                 # int\nratio = 0.5               # float\nname = &quot;Ada&quot;              # str\nready = True              # bool\nresult = None             # explicit absence of a value\nx, y = 2, 5               # unpacking; lengths must match\nx, y = y, x               # swap; right side evaluated first\nfirst, *rest = [1, 2, 3]   # first=1, rest=[2, 3]</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Names are case-sensitive; use <code>snake_case</code>, constants conventionally <code>UPPER_CASE</code>. Names cannot start with a digit or be keywords. Avoid shadowing <code>list</code>, <code>str</code>, <code>sum</code>, etc.</li>\n<li>Dynamic typing: <code>x = 1; x = &quot;one&quot;</code> is legal. Objects have types; names can be rebound to objects of different types.</li>\n<li>Common immutable types: <code>int</code>, <code>float</code>, <code>bool</code>, <code>str</code>, <code>tuple</code>, <code>frozenset</code>. Mutable: <code>list</code>, <code>dict</code>, <code>set</code>.</li>\n<li>Conversion: <code>int(&quot;12&quot;) == 12</code>, <code>str(12) == &quot;12&quot;</code>, <code>int(3.9) == 3</code> (toward zero).</li>\n<li><code>int(&quot;3.9&quot;)</code> raises <code>ValueError</code>; <code>&quot;3&quot; + 2</code> raises <code>TypeError</code>.</li>\n<li><code>None</code> differs from <code>0</code>, <code>False</code>, and <code>&quot;&quot;</code>. Check it with <code>is None</code>.</li>\n<li><code>==</code> compares values; <code>is</code> compares object identity. Do not use <code>is</code> for numeric/string equality.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>a = [1]\nb = a\nb.append(2)       # a and b both refer to [1, 2]\nb = [9]          # rebinding b does not change a</code></pre>\n<p class=\"callout\"><strong>Trap:</strong> <code>a = b = []</code> creates one shared list. Use <code>a, b = [], []</code> for separate lists.</p>", "text": "\n**Names refer to objects. Assignment binds a name; it does not automatically copy an object.**\n\n```python\ncount = 3                 # int\nratio = 0.5               # float\nname = \"Ada\"              # str\nready = True              # bool\nresult = None             # explicit absence of a value\nx, y = 2, 5               # unpacking; lengths must match\nx, y = y, x               # swap; right side evaluated first\nfirst, *rest = [1, 2, 3]   # first=1, rest=[2, 3]\n```\n\n- Names are case-sensitive; use `snake_case`, constants conventionally `UPPER_CASE`. Names cannot start with a digit or be keywords. Avoid shadowing `list`, `str`, `sum`, etc.\n- Dynamic typing: `x = 1; x = \"one\"` is legal. Objects have types; names can be rebound to objects of different types.\n- Common immutable types: `int`, `float`, `bool`, `str`, `tuple`, `frozenset`. Mutable: `list`, `dict`, `set`.\n- Conversion: `int(\"12\") == 12`, `str(12) == \"12\"`, `int(3.9) == 3` (toward zero).\n- `int(\"3.9\")` raises `ValueError`; `\"3\" + 2` raises `TypeError`.\n- `None` differs from `0`, `False`, and `\"\"`. Check it with `is None`.\n- `==` compares values; `is` compares object identity. Do not use `is` for numeric/string equality.\n\n```python\na = [1]\nb = a\nb.append(2)       # a and b both refer to [1, 2]\nb = [9]          # rebinding b does not change a\n```\n\n**Trap:** `a = b = []` creates one shared list. Use `a, b = [], []` for separate lists.\n\n", "minutes": 8, "track": "python", "num": 1}, {"id": "2", "title": "Math and Boolean operators", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Expression</th><th>Result / meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>7 / 2</code></td><td><code>3.5</code> \u2014 true division</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>7 // 2</code></td><td><code>3</code> \u2014 floor division</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>-7 // 2</code></td><td><code>-4</code> \u2014 floors toward negative infinity</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>-7 % 2</code></td><td><code>1</code> \u2014 remainder follows divisor&#x27;s sign</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>2 ** 3</code></td><td><code>8</code> \u2014 exponentiation</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>abs(-4)</code></td><td><code>4</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>divmod(7, 2)</code></td><td><code>(3, 1)</code></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>a == (a // b) * b + a % b</code> for integers and nonzero <code>b</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Shorthand: <code>+=</code>, <code>-=</code>, <code>*=</code>, <code>//=</code>, <code>%=</code>. Python has no increment operator <code>++</code>.</li>\n<li>Parenthesize when unclear. Arithmetic precedes comparisons; Boolean precedence is <code>not</code>, then <code>and</code>, then <code>or</code>.</li>\n<li><code>and</code> / <code>or</code> short-circuit and <strong>return an operand</strong>, not necessarily a Boolean: <code>0 or 5</code> \u2192 <code>5</code>; <code>&quot;a&quot; and 7</code> \u2192 <code>7</code>.</li>\n<li><code>not x</code> always returns a Boolean. <code>items and items[0]</code> avoids indexing an empty list.</li>\n<li><code>&amp;</code>, <code>|</code>, <code>^</code>, <code>~</code>, <code>&lt;&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;&gt;</code> are bitwise operators on integers; they do not replace Boolean <code>and</code>/<code>or</code>.</li>\n<li>Floats have representation error: <code>0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3</code> is false. For approximate comparison, use <code>math.isclose</code> with suitable tolerances.</li>\n<li>Python integers grow as needed; extremely large integer arithmetic is not constant-time.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"callout\"><strong>Trap:</strong> <code>+=</code> can mutate a list, but rebinding an integer/string produces a different object.</p>", "text": "\n| Expression | Result / meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `7 / 2` | `3.5` \u2014 true division |\n| `7 // 2` | `3` \u2014 floor division |\n| `-7 // 2` | `-4` \u2014 floors toward negative infinity |\n| `-7 % 2` | `1` \u2014 remainder follows divisor's sign |\n| `2 ** 3` | `8` \u2014 exponentiation |\n| `abs(-4)` | `4` |\n| `divmod(7, 2)` | `(3, 1)` |\n\n`a == (a // b) * b + a % b` for integers and nonzero `b`.\n\n- Shorthand: `+=`, `-=`, `*=`, `//=`, `%=`. Python has no increment operator `++`.\n- Parenthesize when unclear. Arithmetic precedes comparisons; Boolean precedence is `not`, then `and`, then `or`.\n- `and` / `or` short-circuit and **return an operand**, not necessarily a Boolean: `0 or 5` \u2192 `5`; `\"a\" and 7` \u2192 `7`.\n- `not x` always returns a Boolean. `items and items[0]` avoids indexing an empty list.\n- `&`, `|`, `^`, `~`, `<<`, `>>` are bitwise operators on integers; they do not replace Boolean `and`/`or`.\n- Floats have representation error: `0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3` is false. For approximate comparison, use `math.isclose` with suitable tolerances.\n- Python integers grow as needed; extremely large integer arithmetic is not constant-time.\n\n**Trap:** `+=` can mutate a list, but rebinding an integer/string produces a different object.\n\n", "minutes": 8, "track": "python", "num": 2}, {"id": "3", "title": "Functions", "html": "<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>def scaled_total(values: list[int], factor: int = 2) -&gt; int:\n    return sum(values) * factor\n\nanswer = scaled_total([1, 2], factor=3)   # 9</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Parameters are names in the definition; arguments are the supplied values. Arguments can be positional or keyword; required parameters precede ordinary default parameters.</li>\n<li><code>return</code> exits immediately. No return, or bare <code>return</code>, produces <code>None</code>. Printing is not returning.</li>\n<li>Type hints document intent; Python does not enforce them automatically at runtime.</li>\n<li><code>return low, high</code> returns a tuple, which callers can unpack.</li>\n<li>Arguments bind local names to the supplied objects. Mutating a passed list is visible to the caller; rebinding the parameter is not.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>def update(items):\n    items.append(1)  # changes caller&#x27;s list\n    items = []       # only rebinds the local name</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Scope:</strong> Python searches local \u2192 enclosing functions \u2192 global \u2192 built-ins (LEGB). Assignment inside a function normally makes that name local. Reading it before local assignment raises <code>UnboundLocalError</code>. <code>global</code> allows rebinding a module name; <code>nonlocal</code> allows rebinding an enclosing function&#x27;s name. Prefer explicit parameters and returns in interview solutions.</p>\n<p class=\"callout\"><strong>Critical trap: default arguments are evaluated once, when the function is defined.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>def collect(value, items=None):  # avoid items=[]: shared across calls\n    if items is None:\n        items = []\n    items.append(value)\n    return items</code></pre>\n<p>When <code>*args</code> and <code>**kwargs</code> are used together, Python separates arguments based on how they are passed into the function.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Arguments passed without names go into <code>args</code> (a tuple).</li>\n<li>Arguments passed with names go into <code>kwargs</code> (a dictionary).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>def employee_info(*args, **kwargs):\n    print(&quot;ARGS:&quot;, args)\n    print(&quot;KWARGS:&quot;, kwargs)\n\nemployee_info(101, &quot;Alex&quot;, &quot;Engineer&quot;, age=25, city=&quot;Toronto&quot;)\n# ARGS: (101, &#x27;Alex&#x27;, &#x27;Engineer&#x27;)\n# KWARGS: {&#x27;age&#x27;: 25, &#x27;city&#x27;: &#x27;Toronto&#x27;}</code></pre>\n<p>How Python processes that call:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Incoming argument</th><th>Destination</th><th>Collected as</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>101</code>, <code>&quot;Alex&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;Engineer&quot;</code> (no names)</td><td><code>*args</code> \u2014 positional</td><td><code>(101, &quot;Alex&quot;, &quot;Engineer&quot;)</code> \u2014 tuple</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>age=25</code>, <code>city=&quot;Toronto&quot;</code> (named)</td><td><code>**kwargs</code> \u2014 keyword</td><td><code>{&quot;age&quot;: 25, &quot;city&quot;: &quot;Toronto&quot;}</code> \u2014 dictionary</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>*args</code> must appear before <code>**kwargs</code>. <code>f(*values)</code> unpacks positional arguments; <code>f(**options)</code> unpacks keyword arguments. A small sorting key can use <code>lambda pair: pair[1]</code>.</p>", "text": "\n```python\ndef scaled_total(values: list[int], factor: int = 2) -> int:\n    return sum(values) * factor\n\nanswer = scaled_total([1, 2], factor=3)   # 9\n```\n\n- Parameters are names in the definition; arguments are the supplied values. Arguments can be positional or keyword; required parameters precede ordinary default parameters.\n- `return` exits immediately. No return, or bare `return`, produces `None`. Printing is not returning.\n- Type hints document intent; Python does not enforce them automatically at runtime.\n- `return low, high` returns a tuple, which callers can unpack.\n- Arguments bind local names to the supplied objects. Mutating a passed list is visible to the caller; rebinding the parameter is not.\n\n```python\ndef update(items):\n    items.append(1)  # changes caller's list\n    items = []       # only rebinds the local name\n```\n\n**Scope:** Python searches local \u2192 enclosing functions \u2192 global \u2192 built-ins (LEGB). Assignment inside a function normally makes that name local. Reading it before local assignment raises `UnboundLocalError`. `global` allows rebinding a module name; `nonlocal` allows rebinding an enclosing function's name. Prefer explicit parameters and returns in interview solutions.\n\n**Critical trap: default arguments are evaluated once, when the function is defined.**\n\n```python\ndef collect(value, items=None):  # avoid items=[]: shared across calls\n    if items is None:\n        items = []\n    items.append(value)\n    return items\n```\n\nWhen `*args` and `**kwargs` are used together, Python separates arguments based on how they are passed into the function.\n\n- Arguments passed without names go into `args` (a tuple).\n- Arguments passed with names go into `kwargs` (a dictionary).\n\n```python\ndef employee_info(*args, **kwargs):\n    print(\"ARGS:\", args)\n    print(\"KWARGS:\", kwargs)\n\nemployee_info(101, \"Alex\", \"Engineer\", age=25, city=\"Toronto\")\n# ARGS: (101, 'Alex', 'Engineer')\n# KWARGS: {'age': 25, 'city': 'Toronto'}\n```\n\nHow Python processes that call:\n\n| Incoming argument | Destination | Collected as |\n|---|---|---|\n| `101`, `\"Alex\"`, `\"Engineer\"` (no names) | `*args` \u2014 positional | `(101, \"Alex\", \"Engineer\")` \u2014 tuple |\n| `age=25`, `city=\"Toronto\"` (named) | `**kwargs` \u2014 keyword | `{\"age\": 25, \"city\": \"Toronto\"}` \u2014 dictionary |\n\n`*args` must appear before `**kwargs`. `f(*values)` unpacks positional arguments; `f(**options)` unpacks keyword arguments. A small sorting key can use `lambda pair: pair[1]`.\n\n", "minutes": 10, "track": "python", "num": 3}, {"id": "4", "title": "Conditional statements", "html": "<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>if score &gt;= 80:\n    label = &quot;high&quot;\nelif score &gt;= 50:\n    label = &quot;medium&quot;\nelse:\n    label = &quot;low&quot;\n\nsmaller = a if a &lt; b else b</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>An <code>if</code>/<code>elif</code> chain takes only its first true branch. Separate <code>if</code> statements are evaluated independently.</li>\n<li>Comparisons: <code>==</code>, <code>!=</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&gt;=</code>; chained comparisons such as <code>0 &lt;= i &lt; n</code> are valid.</li>\n<li>Falsy values include <code>None</code>, <code>False</code>, numeric zero, and empty strings/containers. Most other objects are truthy.</li>\n<li><code>if value</code> treats zero/empty as false; <code>if value is not None</code> permits them.</li>\n<li><code>if</code> and <code>for</code> blocks do <strong>not</strong> introduce a new local scope. A name assigned only in a branch may remain undefined if that branch is skipped.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"callout\"><strong>Trap:</strong> <code>if x == 1 or 2:</code> is always truthy. Write <code>if x in (1, 2):</code> or <code>if x == 1 or x == 2:</code>.</p>", "text": "\n```python\nif score >= 80:\n    label = \"high\"\nelif score >= 50:\n    label = \"medium\"\nelse:\n    label = \"low\"\n\nsmaller = a if a < b else b\n```\n\n- An `if`/`elif` chain takes only its first true branch. Separate `if` statements are evaluated independently.\n- Comparisons: `==`, `!=`, `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`; chained comparisons such as `0 <= i < n` are valid.\n- Falsy values include `None`, `False`, numeric zero, and empty strings/containers. Most other objects are truthy.\n- `if value` treats zero/empty as false; `if value is not None` permits them.\n- `if` and `for` blocks do **not** introduce a new local scope. A name assigned only in a branch may remain undefined if that branch is skipped.\n\n**Trap:** `if x == 1 or 2:` is always truthy. Write `if x in (1, 2):` or `if x == 1 or x == 2:`.\n\n", "minutes": 6, "track": "python", "num": 4}, {"id": "5", "title": "Loops", "html": "<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>for value in values:                 # direct iteration\n    print(value)\nfor i, value in enumerate(values):    # index and value\n    print(i, value)\nfor left, right in zip(xs, ys):       # stops at shorter iterable\n    print(left, right)\n\nfor i in range(2, 8, 2):              # 2, 4, 6; stop excluded\n    print(i)\nfor i in range(3, -1, -1):            # 3, 2, 1, 0\n    print(i)\n\nwhile remaining &gt; 0:\n    remaining -= 1                   # ensure progress toward termination</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>break</code>: exit the innermost loop. <code>continue</code>: skip to its next iteration. <code>pass</code>: do nothing.</li>\n<li><code>reversed(values)</code> iterates backward without making a reversed list.</li>\n<li><code>range</code> stores bounds/step rather than building a list. Its step cannot be zero.</li>\n<li>A loop&#x27;s optional <code>else</code> runs when the loop finishes without <code>break</code> (including zero iterations).</li>\n<li>Avoid adding/removing elements from the same collection you are iterating over; build a new collection or iterate over a copy.</li>\n<li>One pass over <code>n</code> elements is O(n); all pairs are O(n\u00b2). Nested loops are not automatically quadratic: count actual iterations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"callout\"><strong>Trap:</strong> <code>continue</code> before a <code>while</code> loop&#x27;s counter update can cause an infinite loop.</p>\n<h3><code>enumerate()</code> \u2014 index + value</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Returns <code>(index, value)</code> pairs while iterating.</li>\n<li>Default index starts at <code>0</code>; change it with <code>start=</code>.</li>\n<li>Useful when you need both the element and its position.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>fruits = [&quot;apple&quot;, &quot;banana&quot;, &quot;cherry&quot;]\nfor i, fruit in enumerate(fruits):\n    print(i, fruit)\n# 0 apple\n# 1 banana\n# 2 cherry\n\nlist(enumerate(fruits, start=1))\n# [(1, &#x27;apple&#x27;), (2, &#x27;banana&#x27;), (3, &#x27;cherry&#x27;)]</code></pre>\n<h3><code>zip()</code> \u2014 combine multiple iterables</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Pairs corresponding elements from two or more iterables.</li>\n<li>Returns tuples.</li>\n<li>Stops at the shortest iterable by default.</li>\n<li><code>strict=True</code> raises <code>ValueError</code> if lengths differ (Python 3.10+).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>names = [&quot;Alex&quot;, &quot;Bob&quot;]\nscores = [85, 90]\nlist(zip(names, scores))\n# [(&#x27;Alex&#x27;, 85), (&#x27;Bob&#x27;, 90)]\n\nfor name, score in zip(names, scores):\n    print(name, score)</code></pre>\n<h3><code>enumerate()</code> + <code>zip()</code> \u2014 index + multiple values</h3>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>for i, (name, score) in enumerate(zip(names, scores)):\n    print(i, name, score)\n# 0 Alex 85\n# 1 Bob 90</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>zip()</code> creates <code>(name, score)</code> pairs.</li>\n<li><code>enumerate()</code> adds an index to each pair.</li>\n<li>Nested unpacking assigns values to <code>i</code>, <code>name</code>, and <code>score</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Useful tricks</h3>\n<p>Create a dictionary:</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>dict(zip([&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;], [1, 2]))\n# {&#x27;a&#x27;: 1, &#x27;b&#x27;: 2}</code></pre>\n<p>Unzip pairs:</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>pairs = [(1, &quot;a&quot;), (2, &quot;b&quot;)]\nnums, letters = zip(*pairs)\n# nums = (1, 2)\n# letters = (&#x27;a&#x27;, &#x27;b&#x27;)</code></pre>\n<p>Find an element&#x27;s index:</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>for i, value in enumerate([10, 20, 30]):\n    if value == 20:\n        print(i)  # 1</code></pre>\n<h3>Related built-in functions</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>range(n)</code></td><td>Integers from <code>0</code> to <code>n-1</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>enumerate(x)</code></td><td>Index + element</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>zip(a, b)</code></td><td>Pair corresponding elements</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>reversed(x)</code></td><td>Iterate backward</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>sorted(x)</code></td><td>Return a sorted list</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>sum(x)</code></td><td>Sum numeric elements</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>any(x)</code></td><td><code>True</code> if at least one is truthy</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>all(x)</code></td><td><code>True</code> if all are truthy</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Interview cheat sheet</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Need only values</td><td><code>for x in arr:</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Need index + value</td><td><code>for i, x in enumerate(arr):</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Need two lists together</td><td><code>for x, y in zip(a, b):</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Need index + two lists</td><td><code>for i, (x, y) in enumerate(zip(a, b)):</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Need a dictionary from two lists</td><td><code>dict(zip(keys, values))</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Need to split pairs into sequences</td><td><code>a, b = zip(*pairs)</code></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Both <code>enumerate()</code> and <code>zip()</code> return lazy iterators: they generate elements on demand rather than building a full list in memory.</p>", "text": "\n```python\nfor value in values:                 # direct iteration\n    print(value)\nfor i, value in enumerate(values):    # index and value\n    print(i, value)\nfor left, right in zip(xs, ys):       # stops at shorter iterable\n    print(left, right)\n\nfor i in range(2, 8, 2):              # 2, 4, 6; stop excluded\n    print(i)\nfor i in range(3, -1, -1):            # 3, 2, 1, 0\n    print(i)\n\nwhile remaining > 0:\n    remaining -= 1                   # ensure progress toward termination\n```\n\n- `break`: exit the innermost loop. `continue`: skip to its next iteration. `pass`: do nothing.\n- `reversed(values)` iterates backward without making a reversed list.\n- `range` stores bounds/step rather than building a list. Its step cannot be zero.\n- A loop's optional `else` runs when the loop finishes without `break` (including zero iterations).\n- Avoid adding/removing elements from the same collection you are iterating over; build a new collection or iterate over a copy.\n- One pass over `n` elements is O(n); all pairs are O(n\u00b2). Nested loops are not automatically quadratic: count actual iterations.\n\n**Trap:** `continue` before a `while` loop's counter update can cause an infinite loop.\n\n### `enumerate()` \u2014 index + value\n\n- Returns `(index, value)` pairs while iterating.\n- Default index starts at `0`; change it with `start=`.\n- Useful when you need both the element and its position.\n\n```python\nfruits = [\"apple\", \"banana\", \"cherry\"]\nfor i, fruit in enumerate(fruits):\n    print(i, fruit)\n# 0 apple\n# 1 banana\n# 2 cherry\n\nlist(enumerate(fruits, start=1))\n# [(1, 'apple'), (2, 'banana'), (3, 'cherry')]\n```\n\n### `zip()` \u2014 combine multiple iterables\n\n- Pairs corresponding elements from two or more iterables.\n- Returns tuples.\n- Stops at the shortest iterable by default.\n- `strict=True` raises `ValueError` if lengths differ (Python 3.10+).\n\n```python\nnames = [\"Alex\", \"Bob\"]\nscores = [85, 90]\nlist(zip(names, scores))\n# [('Alex', 85), ('Bob', 90)]\n\nfor name, score in zip(names, scores):\n    print(name, score)\n```\n\n### `enumerate()` + `zip()` \u2014 index + multiple values\n\n```python\nfor i, (name, score) in enumerate(zip(names, scores)):\n    print(i, name, score)\n# 0 Alex 85\n# 1 Bob 90\n```\n\n- `zip()` creates `(name, score)` pairs.\n- `enumerate()` adds an index to each pair.\n- Nested unpacking assigns values to `i`, `name`, and `score`.\n\n### Useful tricks\n\nCreate a dictionary:\n\n```python\ndict(zip([\"a\", \"b\"], [1, 2]))\n# {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\n```\n\nUnzip pairs:\n\n```python\npairs = [(1, \"a\"), (2, \"b\")]\nnums, letters = zip(*pairs)\n# nums = (1, 2)\n# letters = ('a', 'b')\n```\n\nFind an element's index:\n\n```python\nfor i, value in enumerate([10, 20, 30]):\n    if value == 20:\n        print(i)  # 1\n```\n\n### Related built-in functions\n\n| Function | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `range(n)` | Integers from `0` to `n-1` |\n| `enumerate(x)` | Index + element |\n| `zip(a, b)` | Pair corresponding elements |\n| `reversed(x)` | Iterate backward |\n| `sorted(x)` | Return a sorted list |\n| `sum(x)` | Sum numeric elements |\n| `any(x)` | `True` if at least one is truthy |\n| `all(x)` | `True` if all are truthy |\n\n### Interview cheat sheet\n\n| Requirement | Use |\n|---|---|\n| Need only values | `for x in arr:` |\n| Need index + value | `for i, x in enumerate(arr):` |\n| Need two lists together | `for x, y in zip(a, b):` |\n| Need index + two lists | `for i, (x, y) in enumerate(zip(a, b)):` |\n| Need a dictionary from two lists | `dict(zip(keys, values))` |\n| Need to split pairs into sequences | `a, b = zip(*pairs)` |\n\nBoth `enumerate()` and `zip()` return lazy iterators: they generate elements on demand rather than building a full list in memory.\n\n", "minutes": 12, "track": "python", "num": 5}, {"id": "6", "title": "Strings", "html": "<p>Strings are immutable sequences. Indexing returns a one-character string; there is no separate character type.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>s = &quot;python&quot;\nlen(s)             # 6\ns[0], s[-1]        # (&#x27;p&#x27;, &#x27;n&#x27;)\ns[1:4]            # &#x27;yth&#x27;; start included, stop excluded\ns[::2]            # &#x27;pto&#x27;\ns[::-1]           # &#x27;nohtyp&#x27;\ns[99:]            # &#x27;&#x27;; slicing clips out-of-range bounds\n# s[99]           # IndexError\n# s[0] = &#x27;P&#x27;      # TypeError\ns = &quot;P&quot; + s[1:]    # creates a new string\nf&quot;{s}: {3.14159:.2f}&quot;  # &#x27;Python: 3.14&#x27;</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Iterate directly: <code>for ch in s</code>; use <code>enumerate(s)</code> when you need positions.</li>\n<li><code>s.split()</code> splits on whitespace and collapses repeated whitespace. <code>s.split(&quot;,&quot;)</code> uses a specific separator and preserves empty fields.</li>\n<li><code>&quot;-&quot;.join([&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;])</code> \u2192 <code>&quot;a-b&quot;</code>; all joined elements must be strings.</li>\n<li>Useful methods: <code>strip()</code>, <code>lower()</code>, <code>upper()</code>, <code>replace(old, new)</code>, <code>startswith(prefix)</code>, <code>endswith(suffix)</code>, <code>isalnum()</code>.</li>\n<li><code>s.find(&quot;x&quot;)</code> returns <code>-1</code> when absent; <code>s.index(&quot;x&quot;)</code> raises <code>ValueError</code>.</li>\n<li>Methods return new strings; <code>s.lower()</code> alone does not change <code>s</code>.</li>\n<li><code>ord(&quot;a&quot;)</code> \u2192 <code>97</code>; <code>chr(97)</code> \u2192 <code>&quot;a&quot;</code>. Confirm a problem&#x27;s character set before assuming only lowercase English letters.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"callout\"><strong>Cost:</strong> indexing and <code>len</code> are O(1) in typical Python interview analysis; a slice of length k takes O(k) time/space. Repeatedly copying a growing string can take O(n\u00b2); accumulate pieces in a list and <code>&quot;&quot;.join(parts)</code> for linear total construction.</p>", "text": "\nStrings are immutable sequences. Indexing returns a one-character string; there is no separate character type.\n\n```python\ns = \"python\"\nlen(s)             # 6\ns[0], s[-1]        # ('p', 'n')\ns[1:4]            # 'yth'; start included, stop excluded\ns[::2]            # 'pto'\ns[::-1]           # 'nohtyp'\ns[99:]            # ''; slicing clips out-of-range bounds\n# s[99]           # IndexError\n# s[0] = 'P'      # TypeError\ns = \"P\" + s[1:]    # creates a new string\nf\"{s}: {3.14159:.2f}\"  # 'Python: 3.14'\n```\n\n- Iterate directly: `for ch in s`; use `enumerate(s)` when you need positions.\n- `s.split()` splits on whitespace and collapses repeated whitespace. `s.split(\",\")` uses a specific separator and preserves empty fields.\n- `\"-\".join([\"a\", \"b\"])` \u2192 `\"a-b\"`; all joined elements must be strings.\n- Useful methods: `strip()`, `lower()`, `upper()`, `replace(old, new)`, `startswith(prefix)`, `endswith(suffix)`, `isalnum()`.\n- `s.find(\"x\")` returns `-1` when absent; `s.index(\"x\")` raises `ValueError`.\n- Methods return new strings; `s.lower()` alone does not change `s`.\n- `ord(\"a\")` \u2192 `97`; `chr(97)` \u2192 `\"a\"`. Confirm a problem's character set before assuming only lowercase English letters.\n\n**Cost:** indexing and `len` are O(1) in typical Python interview analysis; a slice of length k takes O(k) time/space. Repeatedly copying a growing string can take O(n\u00b2); accumulate pieces in a list and `\"\".join(parts)` for linear total construction.\n\n", "minutes": 10, "track": "python", "num": 6}, {"id": "7", "title": "Lists and tuples", "html": "<p>Lists are mutable, ordered sequences; duplicates are allowed.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>nums = [3, 1, 2]\nnums.append(4)         # [3, 1, 2, 4]; adds one object\nnums.extend([5, 6])    # adds each element\nlast = nums.pop()     # removes and returns 6\nnums.insert(0, 9)     # inserts before index 0\nnums.remove(1)        # removes first matching value; ValueError if absent\nnums.index(2)         # first matching index; ValueError if absent\ncopy = nums[:]        # shallow copy, like nums.copy()\nsquares = [x * x for x in nums if x &gt; 2]</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Indexing/slicing follow the string rules, but list elements can be reassigned. <code>del nums[i]</code> removes by index.</li>\n<li><code>len</code>, <code>sum</code>, <code>min</code>, <code>max</code> are useful. <code>min([])</code> and <code>max([])</code> raise <code>ValueError</code> unless supplied a <code>default</code>.</li>\n<li><code>nums.sort()</code> sorts in place and returns <code>None</code>; <code>sorted(nums)</code> returns a new list. Both accept <code>key=</code> and <code>reverse=</code>; sorting is stable.</li>\n<li><code>nums.reverse()</code> mutates and returns <code>None</code>; <code>nums[::-1]</code> creates a reversed copy.</li>\n<li><code>append([1, 2])</code> adds one nested list; <code>extend([1, 2])</code> adds two elements.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"callout\"><strong>Critical traps:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>rows = [[0] * 3] * 2  # both entries reference the same row!\nrows = [[0] * 3 for _ in range(2)]  # independent rows</code></pre>\n<h3>Shallow copy vs deep copy</h3>\n<p>Start with a list that contains another list: <code>original = [1, [2, 3]]</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>import copy\n\noriginal = [1, [2, 3]]\nshallow_copied = copy.copy(original)      # same as original[:] or original.copy()\ndeep_copied = copy.deepcopy(original)</code></pre>\n<p>A shallow copy creates a new outer list but reuses the same nested objects. A deep copy recursively duplicates nested structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Modifying the top level:</strong> replacing an element in the copy does not change the original. Both kinds of copy protect <code>original</code> here.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>shallow_copied[0] = 99\n# original        -&gt; [1, [2, 3]]      safe\n# shallow_copied  -&gt; [99, [2, 3]]</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Modifying a nested element:</strong> the difference appears when you mutate the inner list.</p>\n<p>Shallow copy \u2014 shared references. Changing the nested list inside the copy also changes the original:</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>shallow_copied[1][0] = &quot;X&quot;\n# original        -&gt; [1, [&quot;X&quot;, 3]]    modified\n# shallow_copied  -&gt; [99, [&quot;X&quot;, 3]]</code></pre>\n<p>Deep copy \u2014 total independence. The nested list is a new object, so mutating it leaves the original alone:</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>original = [1, [2, 3]]\ndeep_copied = copy.deepcopy(original)\ndeep_copied[1][0] = &quot;Z&quot;\n# original     -&gt; [1, [2, 3]]         safe\n# deep_copied  -&gt; [1, [&quot;Z&quot;, 3]]</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Tuples:</strong> <code>point = (2, 3)</code>; a single-element tuple is <code>(2,)</code>. Tuple slots cannot be reassigned, but a contained mutable object can still change. A tuple can be a dict key/set element only if all its elements are hashable.</p>", "text": "\nLists are mutable, ordered sequences; duplicates are allowed.\n\n```python\nnums = [3, 1, 2]\nnums.append(4)         # [3, 1, 2, 4]; adds one object\nnums.extend([5, 6])    # adds each element\nlast = nums.pop()     # removes and returns 6\nnums.insert(0, 9)     # inserts before index 0\nnums.remove(1)        # removes first matching value; ValueError if absent\nnums.index(2)         # first matching index; ValueError if absent\ncopy = nums[:]        # shallow copy, like nums.copy()\nsquares = [x * x for x in nums if x > 2]\n```\n\n- Indexing/slicing follow the string rules, but list elements can be reassigned. `del nums[i]` removes by index.\n- `len`, `sum`, `min`, `max` are useful. `min([])` and `max([])` raise `ValueError` unless supplied a `default`.\n- `nums.sort()` sorts in place and returns `None`; `sorted(nums)` returns a new list. Both accept `key=` and `reverse=`; sorting is stable.\n- `nums.reverse()` mutates and returns `None`; `nums[::-1]` creates a reversed copy.\n- `append([1, 2])` adds one nested list; `extend([1, 2])` adds two elements.\n\n**Critical traps:**\n\n```python\nrows = [[0] * 3] * 2  # both entries reference the same row!\nrows = [[0] * 3 for _ in range(2)]  # independent rows\n```\n\n### Shallow copy vs deep copy\n\nStart with a list that contains another list: `original = [1, [2, 3]]`.\n\n```python\nimport copy\n\noriginal = [1, [2, 3]]\nshallow_copied = copy.copy(original)      # same as original[:] or original.copy()\ndeep_copied = copy.deepcopy(original)\n```\n\nA shallow copy creates a new outer list but reuses the same nested objects. A deep copy recursively duplicates nested structure.\n\n**Modifying the top level:** replacing an element in the copy does not change the original. Both kinds of copy protect `original` here.\n\n```python\nshallow_copied[0] = 99\n# original        -> [1, [2, 3]]      safe\n# shallow_copied  -> [99, [2, 3]]\n```\n\n**Modifying a nested element:** the difference appears when you mutate the inner list.\n\nShallow copy \u2014 shared references. Changing the nested list inside the copy also changes the original:\n\n```python\nshallow_copied[1][0] = \"X\"\n# original        -> [1, [\"X\", 3]]    modified\n# shallow_copied  -> [99, [\"X\", 3]]\n```\n\nDeep copy \u2014 total independence. The nested list is a new object, so mutating it leaves the original alone:\n\n```python\noriginal = [1, [2, 3]]\ndeep_copied = copy.deepcopy(original)\ndeep_copied[1][0] = \"Z\"\n# original     -> [1, [2, 3]]         safe\n# deep_copied  -> [1, [\"Z\", 3]]\n```\n\n**Tuples:** `point = (2, 3)`; a single-element tuple is `(2,)`. Tuple slots cannot be reassigned, but a contained mutable object can still change. A tuple can be a dict key/set element only if all its elements are hashable.\n\n", "minutes": 14, "track": "python", "num": 7}, {"id": "8", "title": "Sets", "html": "<p>A set stores only unique, hashable elements. It has no positional indexing. Iteration order is not guaranteed.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>values = [1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4]\nunique = set(values)        # {1, 2, 3, 4} \u2014 duplicates removed\nseen = set()                # {} creates a dict!\nseen.add(3)\nseen.update([3, 4])         # {3, 4}\nexists = 3 in seen          # True \u2014 average O(1)</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Automatically removes duplicates.</li>\n<li>Mutable: you can add or remove elements.</li>\n<li>Only hashable elements are allowed: integers, strings, and tuples of hashable values. Lists, dicts, and sets are not.</li>\n<li>Typical uses: membership, duplicate detection, visited nodes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Order is not guaranteed.</strong> You may see <code>{1, 2, 3}</code> for <code>[3, 1, 2, 3, 1]</code>, but do not rely on that.</p>\n<p>To remove duplicates <strong>and</strong> keep first-seen order, use a dict (insertion order is preserved in Python 3.7+):</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>values = [3, 1, 2, 3, 1]\nunique = list(dict.fromkeys(values))    # [3, 1, 2]</code></pre>\n<h3>Set operations</h3>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>A, B = {1, 2, 3, 4}, {3, 4, 5, 6}\nA | B                   # union: {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}\nA &amp; B                   # intersection: {3, 4}\nA - B                   # difference: {1, 2}\nA ^ B                   # symmetric difference: {1, 2, 5, 6}\nA &lt;= B                  # subset: False</code></pre>\n<p>The same operations exist as methods: <code>A.union(B)</code>, <code>A.intersection(B)</code>, <code>A.difference(B)</code>, <code>A.symmetric_difference(B)</code>. Methods accept any iterable; the operators require a set.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Result for A, B above</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Union</td><td>All unique elements</td><td><code>{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Intersection</td><td>Shared elements</td><td><code>{3, 4}</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Difference</td><td>Only in A</td><td><code>{1, 2}</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Symmetric difference</td><td>In either, but not both</td><td><code>{1, 2, 5, 6}</code></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>s = {1, 2, 3}\ns.add(4)                # {1, 2, 3, 4}\ns.remove(2)             # {1, 3, 4}; KeyError if missing\ns.discard(10)           # no error if absent</code></pre>\n<p class=\"callout\"><strong>Trap:</strong> <code>remove(x)</code> raises <code>KeyError</code> when <code>x</code> is missing. <code>discard(x)</code> does nothing.</p>\n<h3><code>frozenset</code> \u2014 immutable set</h3>\n<p>A <code>frozenset</code> cannot be modified after creation. Union and intersection still work; they return a new set rather than mutating.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>s = frozenset([1, 2, 2, 3])     # frozenset({1, 2, 3})\n# s.add(4)                      # AttributeError</code></pre>\n<p>Hashable means the hash value stays stable for the object&#x27;s lifetime, so it can be a dict key or set element. A normal set is mutable, so it is unhashable. A <code>frozenset</code> is hashable when its elements are hashable.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>locations = {frozenset([&quot;Toronto&quot;, &quot;Ottawa&quot;]): &quot;Ontario&quot;}   # works\n# locations = {{&quot;Toronto&quot;, &quot;Ottawa&quot;}: &quot;Ontario&quot;}            # TypeError</code></pre>\n<p><code>frozenset</code> is useful when order does not matter, such as undirected graph edges:</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>connections = {\n    frozenset([&quot;A&quot;, &quot;B&quot;]),\n    frozenset([&quot;B&quot;, &quot;A&quot;]),     # same as frozenset([&quot;A&quot;, &quot;B&quot;])\n    frozenset([&quot;B&quot;, &quot;C&quot;]),\n}\nlen(connections)               # 2</code></pre>\n<h3>set vs frozenset vs list vs tuple</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>list</th><th>tuple</th><th>set</th><th>frozenset</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Preserves order</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No guarantee</td><td>No guarantee</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Allows duplicates</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mutable</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Indexing <code>[0]</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hashable</td><td>No</td><td>If elements are</td><td>No</td><td>If elements are</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Membership</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(n)</td><td>Average O(1)</td><td>Average O(1)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Interview patterns</h3>\n<p>Detect duplicates \u2014 average O(n):</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>def contains_duplicate(nums):\n    return len(nums) != len(set(nums))\n\ncontains_duplicate([1, 2, 3, 2])    # True</code></pre>\n<p>Common elements \u2014 average O(n + m):</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>a, b = [1, 2, 3, 4], [3, 4, 5]\ncommon = set(a) &amp; set(b)            # {3, 4}</code></pre>\n<p>Repeated membership: convert the haystack to a set first. <code>30 in [10, 20, 30, 40]</code> is O(n); <code>30 in {10, 20, 30, 40}</code> is average O(1).</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Remember</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>set(values)</code></td><td>Duplicates removed; order not guaranteed</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>list(dict.fromkeys(values))</code></td><td>Deduplicate, keep first-seen order</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>frozenset(values)</code></td><td>Immutable, hashable set</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>x in s</code></td><td>Average O(1)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>s.add</code> / <code>s.remove</code> / <code>s.discard</code></td><td><code>remove</code> errors if missing</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Union / intersection / difference / symmetric difference</td><td><code>A.union(B)</code>, <code>A.intersection(B)</code>, <code>A.difference(B)</code>, <code>A.symmetric_difference(B)</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>A &lt;= B</code></td><td>A is a subset of B</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Use a set when uniqueness and fast membership matter more than order.</p>", "text": "\nA set stores only unique, hashable elements. It has no positional indexing. Iteration order is not guaranteed.\n\n```python\nvalues = [1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4]\nunique = set(values)        # {1, 2, 3, 4} \u2014 duplicates removed\nseen = set()                # {} creates a dict!\nseen.add(3)\nseen.update([3, 4])         # {3, 4}\nexists = 3 in seen          # True \u2014 average O(1)\n```\n\n- Automatically removes duplicates.\n- Mutable: you can add or remove elements.\n- Only hashable elements are allowed: integers, strings, and tuples of hashable values. Lists, dicts, and sets are not.\n- Typical uses: membership, duplicate detection, visited nodes.\n\n**Order is not guaranteed.** You may see `{1, 2, 3}` for `[3, 1, 2, 3, 1]`, but do not rely on that.\n\nTo remove duplicates **and** keep first-seen order, use a dict (insertion order is preserved in Python 3.7+):\n\n```python\nvalues = [3, 1, 2, 3, 1]\nunique = list(dict.fromkeys(values))    # [3, 1, 2]\n```\n\n### Set operations\n\n```python\nA, B = {1, 2, 3, 4}, {3, 4, 5, 6}\nA | B                   # union: {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}\nA & B                   # intersection: {3, 4}\nA - B                   # difference: {1, 2}\nA ^ B                   # symmetric difference: {1, 2, 5, 6}\nA <= B                  # subset: False\n```\n\nThe same operations exist as methods: `A.union(B)`, `A.intersection(B)`, `A.difference(B)`, `A.symmetric_difference(B)`. Methods accept any iterable; the operators require a set.\n\n| Operation | Meaning | Result for A, B above |\n|---|---|---|\n| Union | All unique elements | `{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}` |\n| Intersection | Shared elements | `{3, 4}` |\n| Difference | Only in A | `{1, 2}` |\n| Symmetric difference | In either, but not both | `{1, 2, 5, 6}` |\n\n```python\ns = {1, 2, 3}\ns.add(4)                # {1, 2, 3, 4}\ns.remove(2)             # {1, 3, 4}; KeyError if missing\ns.discard(10)           # no error if absent\n```\n\n**Trap:** `remove(x)` raises `KeyError` when `x` is missing. `discard(x)` does nothing.\n\n### `frozenset` \u2014 immutable set\n\nA `frozenset` cannot be modified after creation. Union and intersection still work; they return a new set rather than mutating.\n\n```python\ns = frozenset([1, 2, 2, 3])     # frozenset({1, 2, 3})\n# s.add(4)                      # AttributeError\n```\n\nHashable means the hash value stays stable for the object's lifetime, so it can be a dict key or set element. A normal set is mutable, so it is unhashable. A `frozenset` is hashable when its elements are hashable.\n\n```python\nlocations = {frozenset([\"Toronto\", \"Ottawa\"]): \"Ontario\"}   # works\n# locations = {{\"Toronto\", \"Ottawa\"}: \"Ontario\"}            # TypeError\n```\n\n`frozenset` is useful when order does not matter, such as undirected graph edges:\n\n```python\nconnections = {\n    frozenset([\"A\", \"B\"]),\n    frozenset([\"B\", \"A\"]),     # same as frozenset([\"A\", \"B\"])\n    frozenset([\"B\", \"C\"]),\n}\nlen(connections)               # 2\n```\n\n### set vs frozenset vs list vs tuple\n\n| Property | list | tuple | set | frozenset |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Preserves order | Yes | Yes | No guarantee | No guarantee |\n| Allows duplicates | Yes | Yes | No | No |\n| Mutable | Yes | No | Yes | No |\n| Indexing `[0]` | Yes | Yes | No | No |\n| Hashable | No | If elements are | No | If elements are |\n| Membership | O(n) | O(n) | Average O(1) | Average O(1) |\n\n### Interview patterns\n\nDetect duplicates \u2014 average O(n):\n\n```python\ndef contains_duplicate(nums):\n    return len(nums) != len(set(nums))\n\ncontains_duplicate([1, 2, 3, 2])    # True\n```\n\nCommon elements \u2014 average O(n + m):\n\n```python\na, b = [1, 2, 3, 4], [3, 4, 5]\ncommon = set(a) & set(b)            # {3, 4}\n```\n\nRepeated membership: convert the haystack to a set first. `30 in [10, 20, 30, 40]` is O(n); `30 in {10, 20, 30, 40}` is average O(1).\n\n| Concept | Remember |\n|---|---|\n| `set(values)` | Duplicates removed; order not guaranteed |\n| `list(dict.fromkeys(values))` | Deduplicate, keep first-seen order |\n| `frozenset(values)` | Immutable, hashable set |\n| `x in s` | Average O(1) |\n| `s.add` / `s.remove` / `s.discard` | `remove` errors if missing |\n| Union / intersection / difference / symmetric difference | `A.union(B)`, `A.intersection(B)`, `A.difference(B)`, `A.symmetric_difference(B)` |\n| `A <= B` | A is a subset of B |\n\nUse a set when uniqueness and fast membership matter more than order.\n\n", "minutes": 12, "track": "python", "num": 8}, {"id": "9", "title": "Dictionaries", "html": "<p>Dictionaries map unique hashable keys to arbitrary values. Iteration follows insertion order, not sorted order. Assigning an existing key replaces its value.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>counts = {&quot;a&quot;: 2}\ncounts[&quot;b&quot;] = 1\ncounts[&quot;a&quot;] += 1\ncounts.get(&quot;missing&quot;, 0)   # 0; does not insert the key\ncounts[&quot;c&quot;] = counts.get(&quot;c&quot;, 0) + 1\n\nfor key, value in counts.items():\n    print(key, value)\n\nremoved = counts.pop(&quot;b&quot;)\ncounts.pop(&quot;missing&quot;, None)  # default avoids KeyError\n# del counts[&#x27;a&#x27;]            # KeyError if absent</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>key in d</code> checks <strong>keys</strong>. <code>value in d.values()</code> checks values and takes a linear scan.</li>\n<li><code>d[key]</code> raises <code>KeyError</code> when absent. <code>get</code> is useful when absence is expected.</li>\n<li><code>keys()</code>, <code>values()</code>, and <code>items()</code> return dynamic views; use <code>list(...)</code> for a snapshot.</li>\n<li><code>d.update(other)</code> merges, overwriting duplicate keys. <code>d.copy()</code> is shallow.</li>\n<li>Do not change dict size while iterating over it.</li>\n<li><code>True</code> and <code>1</code> compare equal and have the same hash, so they represent the same dictionary key.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"callout\"><strong>Trap:</strong> <code>d.get(key)</code> returning <code>None</code> cannot distinguish a missing key from a stored <code>None</code>. Use <code>key in d</code> when that distinction matters.</p>\n<h3><code>Counter</code> \u2014 automatic frequency counting</h3>\n<p><code>Counter</code> counts how often hashable items appear. It can take a string, list, or other iterable.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>from collections import Counter\n\nword = &quot;banana&quot;\nfreq = {}\nfor char in word:\n    freq[char] = freq.get(char, 0) + 1\n# {&#x27;b&#x27;: 1, &#x27;a&#x27;: 3, &#x27;n&#x27;: 2}\n\nfreq = Counter(&quot;banana&quot;)\n# Counter({&#x27;a&#x27;: 3, &#x27;n&#x27;: 2, &#x27;b&#x27;: 1})</code></pre>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Character</th><th>Count</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>a</code></td><td>3</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>n</code></td><td>2</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>b</code></td><td>1</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>freq[&quot;a&quot;]               # 3\nfreq[&quot;z&quot;]               # 0 \u2014 missing key; does not insert\nfreq.most_common(2)     # [(&#x27;a&#x27;, 3), (&#x27;n&#x27;, 2)]\nfreq.update(&quot;aaa&quot;)\nfreq[&quot;a&quot;]               # 6</code></pre>\n<p>A regular dict raises <code>KeyError</code> on <code>d[&quot;z&quot;]</code>. <code>Counter</code> returns <code>0</code> and does <strong>not</strong> insert the key.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>nums = [1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3]\nCounter(nums)           # Counter({3: 3, 2: 2, 1: 1})</code></pre>\n<p>Valid anagram \u2014 same frequencies, average O(n + m):</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>def is_anagram(s, t):\n    return Counter(s) == Counter(t)\n\nis_anagram(&quot;listen&quot;, &quot;silent&quot;)  # True\nis_anagram(&quot;hello&quot;, &quot;world&quot;)    # False</code></pre>\n<h3><code>defaultdict</code> \u2014 initialize missing keys</h3>\n<p><code>d[&quot;a&quot;].append(0)</code> on a normal dict raises <code>KeyError</code> because <code>&quot;a&quot;</code> does not exist yet. A <code>defaultdict</code> creates the default, stores it, then continues.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>from collections import defaultdict\n\npositions = defaultdict(list)\npositions[&quot;a&quot;].append(0)\n# defaultdict(&lt;class &#x27;list&#x27;&gt;, {&#x27;a&#x27;: [0]})</code></pre>\n<p>What happens on <code>positions[&quot;a&quot;].append(0)</code> when <code>&quot;a&quot;</code> is missing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Python checks whether <code>&quot;a&quot;</code> exists.</li>\n<li>It does not.</li>\n<li><code>defaultdict(list)</code> calls <code>list()</code>, which creates <code>[]</code>.</li>\n<li>It stores <code>&quot;a&quot;: []</code>.</li>\n<li><code>.append(0)</code> adds <code>0</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Constructor</th><th>Default</th><th>Common use</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>defaultdict(list)</code></td><td><code>[]</code></td><td>Group elements</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>defaultdict(int)</code></td><td><code>0</code></td><td>Count frequencies</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>defaultdict(set)</code></td><td><code>set()</code></td><td>Unique grouped values</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>defaultdict(float)</code></td><td><code>0.0</code></td><td>Numeric accumulation</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>freq = defaultdict(int)\nfor char in &quot;banana&quot;:\n    freq[char] += 1             # missing key starts at 0\n\npositions = defaultdict(list)\nfor i, char in enumerate(&quot;banana&quot;):\n    positions[char].append(i)\n# {&#x27;b&#x27;: [0], &#x27;a&#x27;: [1, 3, 5], &#x27;n&#x27;: [2, 4]}\n\nedges = [(&quot;A&quot;, &quot;B&quot;), (&quot;A&quot;, &quot;C&quot;), (&quot;A&quot;, &quot;B&quot;)]\ngraph = defaultdict(set)\nfor u, v in edges:\n    graph[u].add(v)\n# {&#x27;A&#x27;: {&#x27;B&#x27;, &#x27;C&#x27;}} \u2014 duplicate edge stored once</code></pre>\n<p><code>[]</code> on a missing <code>defaultdict</code> key <strong>inserts</strong> the default. <code>.get()</code> does not:</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>d = defaultdict(list)\nd.get(&quot;missing&quot;)        # None \u2014 list is not created\nd[&quot;missing&quot;]            # [] \u2014 created and stored</code></pre>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>dict</th><th>defaultdict</th><th>Counter</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Missing key with <code>[]</code></td><td><code>KeyError</code></td><td>Creates default</td><td>Returns 0</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Counts an iterable automatically</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Groups into lists</td><td>Manual</td><td>Easy</td><td>Not the job</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>most_common()</code></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Interview rule: frequencies \u2192 <code>Counter</code>; grouping \u2192 <code>defaultdict(list)</code>; counting in a loop \u2192 <code>defaultdict(int)</code> or <code>Counter</code>; unique neighbors \u2192 <code>defaultdict(set)</code>. Lookups and updates are average O(1).</p>\n<p>Other <code>collections</code> tools you will meet: <code>deque</code> (both ends), <code>namedtuple</code> (named fields), <code>ChainMap</code> (layered lookup). For interviews, <code>Counter</code>, <code>defaultdict</code>, and <code>deque</code> matter most.</p>", "text": "\nDictionaries map unique hashable keys to arbitrary values. Iteration follows insertion order, not sorted order. Assigning an existing key replaces its value.\n\n```python\ncounts = {\"a\": 2}\ncounts[\"b\"] = 1\ncounts[\"a\"] += 1\ncounts.get(\"missing\", 0)   # 0; does not insert the key\ncounts[\"c\"] = counts.get(\"c\", 0) + 1\n\nfor key, value in counts.items():\n    print(key, value)\n\nremoved = counts.pop(\"b\")\ncounts.pop(\"missing\", None)  # default avoids KeyError\n# del counts['a']            # KeyError if absent\n```\n\n- `key in d` checks **keys**. `value in d.values()` checks values and takes a linear scan.\n- `d[key]` raises `KeyError` when absent. `get` is useful when absence is expected.\n- `keys()`, `values()`, and `items()` return dynamic views; use `list(...)` for a snapshot.\n- `d.update(other)` merges, overwriting duplicate keys. `d.copy()` is shallow.\n- Do not change dict size while iterating over it.\n- `True` and `1` compare equal and have the same hash, so they represent the same dictionary key.\n\n**Trap:** `d.get(key)` returning `None` cannot distinguish a missing key from a stored `None`. Use `key in d` when that distinction matters.\n\n### `Counter` \u2014 automatic frequency counting\n\n`Counter` counts how often hashable items appear. It can take a string, list, or other iterable.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\nword = \"banana\"\nfreq = {}\nfor char in word:\n    freq[char] = freq.get(char, 0) + 1\n# {'b': 1, 'a': 3, 'n': 2}\n\nfreq = Counter(\"banana\")\n# Counter({'a': 3, 'n': 2, 'b': 1})\n```\n\n| Character | Count |\n|---|---|\n| `a` | 3 |\n| `n` | 2 |\n| `b` | 1 |\n\n```python\nfreq[\"a\"]               # 3\nfreq[\"z\"]               # 0 \u2014 missing key; does not insert\nfreq.most_common(2)     # [('a', 3), ('n', 2)]\nfreq.update(\"aaa\")\nfreq[\"a\"]               # 6\n```\n\nA regular dict raises `KeyError` on `d[\"z\"]`. `Counter` returns `0` and does **not** insert the key.\n\n```python\nnums = [1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3]\nCounter(nums)           # Counter({3: 3, 2: 2, 1: 1})\n```\n\nValid anagram \u2014 same frequencies, average O(n + m):\n\n```python\ndef is_anagram(s, t):\n    return Counter(s) == Counter(t)\n\nis_anagram(\"listen\", \"silent\")  # True\nis_anagram(\"hello\", \"world\")    # False\n```\n\n### `defaultdict` \u2014 initialize missing keys\n\n`d[\"a\"].append(0)` on a normal dict raises `KeyError` because `\"a\"` does not exist yet. A `defaultdict` creates the default, stores it, then continues.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\npositions = defaultdict(list)\npositions[\"a\"].append(0)\n# defaultdict(<class 'list'>, {'a': [0]})\n```\n\nWhat happens on `positions[\"a\"].append(0)` when `\"a\"` is missing:\n\n- Python checks whether `\"a\"` exists.\n- It does not.\n- `defaultdict(list)` calls `list()`, which creates `[]`.\n- It stores `\"a\": []`.\n- `.append(0)` adds `0`.\n\n| Constructor | Default | Common use |\n|---|---|---|\n| `defaultdict(list)` | `[]` | Group elements |\n| `defaultdict(int)` | `0` | Count frequencies |\n| `defaultdict(set)` | `set()` | Unique grouped values |\n| `defaultdict(float)` | `0.0` | Numeric accumulation |\n\n```python\nfreq = defaultdict(int)\nfor char in \"banana\":\n    freq[char] += 1             # missing key starts at 0\n\npositions = defaultdict(list)\nfor i, char in enumerate(\"banana\"):\n    positions[char].append(i)\n# {'b': [0], 'a': [1, 3, 5], 'n': [2, 4]}\n\nedges = [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"A\", \"C\"), (\"A\", \"B\")]\ngraph = defaultdict(set)\nfor u, v in edges:\n    graph[u].add(v)\n# {'A': {'B', 'C'}} \u2014 duplicate edge stored once\n```\n\n`[]` on a missing `defaultdict` key **inserts** the default. `.get()` does not:\n\n```python\nd = defaultdict(list)\nd.get(\"missing\")        # None \u2014 list is not created\nd[\"missing\"]            # [] \u2014 created and stored\n```\n\n| Feature | dict | defaultdict | Counter |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Missing key with `[]` | `KeyError` | Creates default | Returns 0 |\n| Counts an iterable automatically | No | No | Yes |\n| Groups into lists | Manual | Easy | Not the job |\n| `most_common()` | No | No | Yes |\n\nInterview rule: frequencies \u2192 `Counter`; grouping \u2192 `defaultdict(list)`; counting in a loop \u2192 `defaultdict(int)` or `Counter`; unique neighbors \u2192 `defaultdict(set)`. Lookups and updates are average O(1).\n\nOther `collections` tools you will meet: `deque` (both ends), `namedtuple` (named fields), `ChainMap` (layered lookup). For interviews, `Counter`, `defaultdict`, and `deque` matter most.\n\n", "minutes": 14, "track": "python", "num": 9}, {"id": "10", "title": "Reading stdin", "html": "<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>name = input()                        # str, trailing newline removed\nn = int(input())                      # explicit conversion\nnums = list(map(int, input().split())) # space-separated integers\na, b = map(int, input().split())       # exactly two values required</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>input()</code> returns a string even when the input looks numeric. End-of-file raises <code>EOFError</code>.</li>\n<li><code>map</code> is a lazy iterator; use <code>list(...)</code> if you need indexing or repeated traversal.</li>\n<li>For larger input, <code>sys.stdin.readline()</code> keeps the newline and returns <code>&quot;&quot;</code> at EOF. Use <code>.rstrip(&quot;\\n&quot;)</code> if spaces must be preserved.</li>\n<li>Many live interview platforms call your function directly: follow the provided signature and return values. Parse stdin only when required.</li>\n<li><code>print(*nums)</code> prints elements separated by spaces; avoid extra debug output in judged submissions.</li>\n</ul>", "text": "\n```python\nname = input()                        # str, trailing newline removed\nn = int(input())                      # explicit conversion\nnums = list(map(int, input().split())) # space-separated integers\na, b = map(int, input().split())       # exactly two values required\n```\n\n- `input()` returns a string even when the input looks numeric. End-of-file raises `EOFError`.\n- `map` is a lazy iterator; use `list(...)` if you need indexing or repeated traversal.\n- For larger input, `sys.stdin.readline()` keeps the newline and returns `\"\"` at EOF. Use `.rstrip(\"\\n\")` if spaces must be preserved.\n- Many live interview platforms call your function directly: follow the provided signature and return values. Parse stdin only when required.\n- `print(*nums)` prints elements separated by spaces; avoid extra debug output in judged submissions.\n\n", "minutes": 5, "track": "python", "num": 10}, {"id": "11", "title": "Exception handling", "html": "<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>def parse_integer(text):\n    try:\n        value = int(text)\n    except ValueError as exc:\n        raise ValueError(&quot;Expected an integer&quot;) from exc\n    else:\n        return value       # runs if try succeeds\n    finally:\n        pass               # cleanup on normal return or exception</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Catch specific expected exceptions; order handlers from specific to general.</li>\n<li>Use <code>except (ValueError, TypeError):</code> when handling both identically, or separate handlers for different recovery.</li>\n<li><code>raise</code> inside a handler re-raises the current exception. <code>raise ValueError(&quot;message&quot;)</code> signals invalid input.</li>\n<li>Avoid swallowing errors with bare <code>except:</code> or <code>except Exception: pass</code>; this hides defects.</li>\n<li>Avoid <code>return</code> in <code>finally</code>: it can override an earlier return or suppress an exception.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Exception</th><th>Typical trigger</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>TypeError</code></td><td>Unsupported operation/type; unhashable key</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>ValueError</code></td><td>Correct kind of argument, invalid value; failed conversion</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>IndexError</code></td><td>Sequence index outside bounds</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>KeyError</code></td><td>Missing dictionary key / set removal</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>NameError</code></td><td>Name not defined</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>UnboundLocalError</code></td><td>Local name read before assignment</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>ZeroDivisionError</code></td><td>Division or modulo by zero</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>", "text": "\n```python\ndef parse_integer(text):\n    try:\n        value = int(text)\n    except ValueError as exc:\n        raise ValueError(\"Expected an integer\") from exc\n    else:\n        return value       # runs if try succeeds\n    finally:\n        pass               # cleanup on normal return or exception\n```\n\n- Catch specific expected exceptions; order handlers from specific to general.\n- Use `except (ValueError, TypeError):` when handling both identically, or separate handlers for different recovery.\n- `raise` inside a handler re-raises the current exception. `raise ValueError(\"message\")` signals invalid input.\n- Avoid swallowing errors with bare `except:` or `except Exception: pass`; this hides defects.\n- Avoid `return` in `finally`: it can override an earlier return or suppress an exception.\n\n| Exception | Typical trigger |\n|---|---|\n| `TypeError` | Unsupported operation/type; unhashable key |\n| `ValueError` | Correct kind of argument, invalid value; failed conversion |\n| `IndexError` | Sequence index outside bounds |\n| `KeyError` | Missing dictionary key / set removal |\n| `NameError` | Name not defined |\n| `UnboundLocalError` | Local name read before assignment |\n| `ZeroDivisionError` | Division or modulo by zero |\n\n", "minutes": 7, "track": "python", "num": 11}, {"id": "12", "title": "Costs to remember before DSA", "html": "<p>Assume n elements and ordinary fixed-size values; hash-table costs are expected/average, with O(n) worst cases for individual lookups/updates.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Time</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>List index / assignment / <code>len</code></td><td>O(1)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>List append</td><td>O(1) amortized</td></tr>\n<tr><td>List pop from end</td><td>O(1)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>List insert/remove/pop at front</td><td>O(n)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>List membership / index / min / max / sum</td><td>O(n)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>List slice or shallow copy of k elements</td><td>O(k), also O(k) extra space</td></tr>\n<tr><td>List sorting</td><td>O(n log n) worst case</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dict/set membership, lookup, insertion, deletion</td><td>O(1) average; O(n) worst case</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Iterate list/dict/set</td><td>O(n)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Hashing/comparing long keys adds work; do not assume a newly created long string/tuple key costs O(1) to process.</p>", "text": "\nAssume n elements and ordinary fixed-size values; hash-table costs are expected/average, with O(n) worst cases for individual lookups/updates.\n\n| Operation | Time |\n|---|---|\n| List index / assignment / `len` | O(1) |\n| List append | O(1) amortized |\n| List pop from end | O(1) |\n| List insert/remove/pop at front | O(n) |\n| List membership / index / min / max / sum | O(n) |\n| List slice or shallow copy of k elements | O(k), also O(k) extra space |\n| List sorting | O(n log n) worst case |\n| Dict/set membership, lookup, insertion, deletion | O(1) average; O(n) worst case |\n| Iterate list/dict/set | O(n) |\n\nHashing/comparing long keys adds work; do not assume a newly created long string/tuple key costs O(1) to process.\n\n", "minutes": 6, "track": "python", "num": 12}, {"id": "13", "title": "Ready to move on", "html": "<p>You should be able to explain these without looking them up:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Assignment versus mutation versus shallow copying versus <code>deepcopy</code>.</li>\n<li>Equality versus identity; truthiness versus checking for <code>None</code>.</li>\n<li>Floor division with negatives and short-circuit evaluation.</li>\n<li>Return values, local scope, and the mutable-default pitfall.</li>\n<li>Exclusive slice/range bounds and safe reverse iteration.</li>\n<li><code>enumerate</code> vs <code>zip</code>, and <code>enumerate(zip(...))</code> unpacking.</li>\n<li><code>append</code> versus <code>extend</code>; <code>sort</code> versus <code>sorted</code>.</li>\n<li>Hashability, dictionary membership, and missing-key handling.</li>\n<li><code>Counter</code> vs <code>defaultdict</code> vs <code>dict</code>, including <code>[]</code> vs <code>.get()</code>.</li>\n<li><code>set</code> vs <code>frozenset</code>, <code>remove</code> vs <code>discard</code>, and <code>list(dict.fromkeys(...))</code> for ordered uniques.</li>\n<li>Why list membership and front removal can make an otherwise simple algorithm slow.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Next bridge topics: Big-O, recursion/call stacks, <code>deque</code>, <code>heapq</code>, <code>bisect</code>, and small node classes. These belong to the DSA phase rather than this basics revision.</p>", "text": "\nYou should be able to explain these without looking them up:\n\n- Assignment versus mutation versus shallow copying versus `deepcopy`.\n- Equality versus identity; truthiness versus checking for `None`.\n- Floor division with negatives and short-circuit evaluation.\n- Return values, local scope, and the mutable-default pitfall.\n- Exclusive slice/range bounds and safe reverse iteration.\n- `enumerate` vs `zip`, and `enumerate(zip(...))` unpacking.\n- `append` versus `extend`; `sort` versus `sorted`.\n- Hashability, dictionary membership, and missing-key handling.\n- `Counter` vs `defaultdict` vs `dict`, including `[]` vs `.get()`.\n- `set` vs `frozenset`, `remove` vs `discard`, and `list(dict.fromkeys(...))` for ordered uniques.\n- Why list membership and front removal can make an otherwise simple algorithm slow.\n\nNext bridge topics: Big-O, recursion/call stacks, `deque`, `heapq`, `bisect`, and small node classes. These belong to the DSA phase rather than this basics revision.\n", "minutes": 3, "track": "python", "num": 13}, {"id": "i1", "title": "Sorting", "html": "<p><code>list.sort()</code> mutates in place and returns <code>None</code>. <code>sorted(iterable)</code> returns a new list and leaves the original alone. Both are Timsort: O(n log n) time, O(n) extra space, and <strong>stable</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>nums = [5, 1, 4, 2]\nnums.sort()                 # nums is now [1, 2, 4, 5]; expression value is None\nnums.sort(reverse=True)     # [5, 4, 2, 1]</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Custom key:</strong> sort by a derived value. The <code>key</code> function runs once per element, then those keys are compared.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>words = [&quot;pear&quot;, &quot;fig&quot;, &quot;apple&quot;]\nwords.sort(key=len)                         # [&#x27;fig&#x27;, &#x27;pear&#x27;, &#x27;apple&#x27;]\npairs = [(1, 3), (2, 1), (1, 2)]\npairs.sort(key=lambda p: (p[0], -p[1]))     # [(1, 3), (1, 2), (2, 1)]</code></pre>\n<p><code>lambda</code> is a one-line anonymous function. Prefer <code>key=len</code> or <code>key=str.lower</code> over a lambda when a named function already exists.</p>\n<p><code>sorted</code> is the copy:</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>original = [3, 1, 2]\ncopy = sorted(original)     # [1, 2, 3]\n# original is still [3, 1, 2]</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>key=</code> is the usual interview tool. <code>cmp</code> comparators are gone in Python 3.</li>\n<li>Tuples compare left to right, so <code>key=lambda x: (x[0], -x[1])</code> sorts by first field ascending, second descending.</li>\n<li>Sorting is stable: equal keys keep their original relative order.</li>\n<li><code>sort</code>/<code>sorted</code> compare values; mixing incomparable types (<code>1</code> and <code>&quot;a&quot;</code>) raises <code>TypeError</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"callout\"><strong>Trap:</strong> <code>nums = nums.sort()</code> sets <code>nums</code> to <code>None</code>. Call <code>nums.sort()</code> as a statement, or use <code>nums = sorted(nums)</code>.</p>", "text": "\n`list.sort()` mutates in place and returns `None`. `sorted(iterable)` returns a new list and leaves the original alone. Both are Timsort: O(n log n) time, O(n) extra space, and **stable**.\n\n```python\nnums = [5, 1, 4, 2]\nnums.sort()                 # nums is now [1, 2, 4, 5]; expression value is None\nnums.sort(reverse=True)     # [5, 4, 2, 1]\n```\n\n**Custom key:** sort by a derived value. The `key` function runs once per element, then those keys are compared.\n\n```python\nwords = [\"pear\", \"fig\", \"apple\"]\nwords.sort(key=len)                         # ['fig', 'pear', 'apple']\npairs = [(1, 3), (2, 1), (1, 2)]\npairs.sort(key=lambda p: (p[0], -p[1]))     # [(1, 3), (1, 2), (2, 1)]\n```\n\n`lambda` is a one-line anonymous function. Prefer `key=len` or `key=str.lower` over a lambda when a named function already exists.\n\n`sorted` is the copy:\n\n```python\noriginal = [3, 1, 2]\ncopy = sorted(original)     # [1, 2, 3]\n# original is still [3, 1, 2]\n```\n\n- `key=` is the usual interview tool. `cmp` comparators are gone in Python 3.\n- Tuples compare left to right, so `key=lambda x: (x[0], -x[1])` sorts by first field ascending, second descending.\n- Sorting is stable: equal keys keep their original relative order.\n- `sort`/`sorted` compare values; mixing incomparable types (`1` and `\"a\"`) raises `TypeError`.\n\n**Trap:** `nums = nums.sort()` sets `nums` to `None`. Call `nums.sort()` as a statement, or use `nums = sorted(nums)`.\n\n", "minutes": 10, "track": "interview", "num": 1}, {"id": "i2", "title": "Pythonic code", "html": "<p><strong>Unpacking</strong> binds names from a sequence. Lengths must match unless you use a starred target.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>a, b = 2, 5\na, b = b, a                 # swap; right side is evaluated first\nfirst, *rest = [1, 2, 3, 4] # first=1, rest=[2, 3, 4]\nleft, *mid, right = [1, 2, 3, 4]  # left=1, mid=[2, 3], right=4</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Loop unpacking</strong> works the same way on each element:</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>for x, y in [(1, 2), (3, 4)]:\n    print(x + y)            # 3, then 7</code></pre>\n<p>**<code>enumerate</code>** yields <code>(index, value)</code>. Default start is <code>0</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>for i, ch in enumerate(&quot;ab&quot;):\n    print(i, ch)            # 0 a, then 1 b</code></pre>\n<p>**<code>zip</code>** pairs corresponding elements and stops at the shortest iterable.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>for name, score in zip([&quot;Ada&quot;, &quot;Bob&quot;], [90, 85]):\n    print(name, score)</code></pre>\n<p><code>enumerate(zip(a, b))</code> needs nested unpacking: <code>for i, (x, y) in enumerate(zip(a, b)):</code>. Both <code>enumerate</code> and <code>zip</code> are lazy iterators.</p>\n<p><strong>Chained inequality</strong> is valid and reads like math. Each value is evaluated once.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>if 0 &lt;= i &lt; n:              # not: if 0 &lt;= i and i &lt; n, though that is equivalent\n    pass</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Min/max shortcut:</strong> <code>min</code> and <code>max</code> accept an iterable or several arguments, plus <code>key=</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>best = min(3, 1, 4)         # 1\nbest = min(pairs, key=lambda p: p[1])\n# empty iterable raises ValueError unless you pass default=</code></pre>\n<p>Interview habit: update a running extreme with <code>best = min(best, candidate)</code> instead of an <code>if</code>.</p>\n<p class=\"callout\"><strong>Trap:</strong> <code>if x == 1 or 2:</code> is always truthy. Write <code>if x in (1, 2):</code>. <code>zip</code> silently drops leftover items unless you use <code>strict=True</code> (Python 3.10+).</p>", "text": "\n**Unpacking** binds names from a sequence. Lengths must match unless you use a starred target.\n\n```python\na, b = 2, 5\na, b = b, a                 # swap; right side is evaluated first\nfirst, *rest = [1, 2, 3, 4] # first=1, rest=[2, 3, 4]\nleft, *mid, right = [1, 2, 3, 4]  # left=1, mid=[2, 3], right=4\n```\n\n**Loop unpacking** works the same way on each element:\n\n```python\nfor x, y in [(1, 2), (3, 4)]:\n    print(x + y)            # 3, then 7\n```\n\n**`enumerate`** yields `(index, value)`. Default start is `0`.\n\n```python\nfor i, ch in enumerate(\"ab\"):\n    print(i, ch)            # 0 a, then 1 b\n```\n\n**`zip`** pairs corresponding elements and stops at the shortest iterable.\n\n```python\nfor name, score in zip([\"Ada\", \"Bob\"], [90, 85]):\n    print(name, score)\n```\n\n`enumerate(zip(a, b))` needs nested unpacking: `for i, (x, y) in enumerate(zip(a, b)):`. Both `enumerate` and `zip` are lazy iterators.\n\n**Chained inequality** is valid and reads like math. Each value is evaluated once.\n\n```python\nif 0 <= i < n:              # not: if 0 <= i and i < n, though that is equivalent\n    pass\n```\n\n**Min/max shortcut:** `min` and `max` accept an iterable or several arguments, plus `key=`.\n\n```python\nbest = min(3, 1, 4)         # 1\nbest = min(pairs, key=lambda p: p[1])\n# empty iterable raises ValueError unless you pass default=\n```\n\nInterview habit: update a running extreme with `best = min(best, candidate)` instead of an `if`.\n\n**Trap:** `if x == 1 or 2:` is always truthy. Write `if x in (1, 2):`. `zip` silently drops leftover items unless you use `strict=True` (Python 3.10+).\n\n", "minutes": 8, "track": "interview", "num": 2}, {"id": "i3", "title": "Lists", "html": "<p>A Python list is a resizable dynamic array. Indexing and assignment are O(1). <code>append</code> / <code>pop()</code> at the end are O(1) amortized. Insert, remove, or <code>pop(0)</code> at the front are O(n).</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>arr = []\narr.append(3)               # [3]\narr.append(5)               # [3, 5]\nlast = arr.pop()            # 5; arr is [3]\narr.insert(0, 9)            # [9, 3] \u2014 O(n)</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Concatenation</strong> creates a new list. <code>extend</code> / <code>+=</code> mutate.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>left, right = [1, 2], [3]\ncombined = left + right     # [1, 2, 3]; left unchanged\nleft.extend(right)          # left is [1, 2, 3]\n# left.append(right)        # would add one nested list: [1, 2, [3]]</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Initialization:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>zeros = [0] * 4             # [0, 0, 0, 0] \u2014 fine for immutable elements\n# rows = [[0] * 3] * 2      # both rows share one inner list\nrows = [[0] * 3 for _ in range(2)]</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Clone:</strong> <code>arr[:]</code> and <code>arr.copy()</code> are shallow. Assignment shares the same list.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>import copy\n\noriginal = [1, [2, 3]]\nalias = original            # same object\nshallow = original[:]       # new outer list, same inner list\ndeep = copy.deepcopy(original)</code></pre>\n<p><strong>The scenario.</strong> <code>original = [1, [2, 3]]</code>.</p>\n<p>Replacing a top-level element only changes that copy. Both shallow and deep copies protect the original here:</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>shallow[0] = 99\n# original -&gt; [1, [2, 3]]     safe\n# shallow  -&gt; [99, [2, 3]]</code></pre>\n<p>Mutating the nested list is where they differ. A shallow copy still shares that inner list:</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>shallow[1][0] = &quot;X&quot;\n# original -&gt; [1, [&quot;X&quot;, 3]]   modified\n# shallow  -&gt; [99, [&quot;X&quot;, 3]]</code></pre>\n<p>A deep copy duplicates nested structure, so the original stays independent:</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>original = [1, [2, 3]]\ndeep = copy.deepcopy(original)\ndeep[1][0] = &quot;Z&quot;\n# original -&gt; [1, [2, 3]]     safe\n# deep     -&gt; [1, [&quot;Z&quot;, 3]]</code></pre>\n<p><strong>List comprehension</strong> builds a new list in one expression.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>squares = [x * x for x in range(5) if x % 2 == 0]   # [0, 4, 16]</code></pre>\n<p>Prefer a comprehension when the result is a list you will keep. Prefer a loop when you are only iterating, or when the body is several statements.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Time</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Index / assign / <code>len</code> / <code>append</code> / <code>pop()</code></td><td>O(1) (<code>append</code> amortized)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>pop(0)</code>, <code>insert(0, x)</code>, <code>remove</code>, <code>in</code></td><td>O(n)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>arr + other</code>, <code>arr[:]</code>, comprehension of n items</td><td>O(n)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"callout\"><strong>Trap:</strong> <code>a = b = []</code> creates one shared list. Use <code>a, b = [], []</code>.</p>", "text": "\nA Python list is a resizable dynamic array. Indexing and assignment are O(1). `append` / `pop()` at the end are O(1) amortized. Insert, remove, or `pop(0)` at the front are O(n).\n\n```python\narr = []\narr.append(3)               # [3]\narr.append(5)               # [3, 5]\nlast = arr.pop()            # 5; arr is [3]\narr.insert(0, 9)            # [9, 3] \u2014 O(n)\n```\n\n**Concatenation** creates a new list. `extend` / `+=` mutate.\n\n```python\nleft, right = [1, 2], [3]\ncombined = left + right     # [1, 2, 3]; left unchanged\nleft.extend(right)          # left is [1, 2, 3]\n# left.append(right)        # would add one nested list: [1, 2, [3]]\n```\n\n**Initialization:**\n\n```python\nzeros = [0] * 4             # [0, 0, 0, 0] \u2014 fine for immutable elements\n# rows = [[0] * 3] * 2      # both rows share one inner list\nrows = [[0] * 3 for _ in range(2)]\n```\n\n**Clone:** `arr[:]` and `arr.copy()` are shallow. Assignment shares the same list.\n\n```python\nimport copy\n\noriginal = [1, [2, 3]]\nalias = original            # same object\nshallow = original[:]       # new outer list, same inner list\ndeep = copy.deepcopy(original)\n```\n\n**The scenario.** `original = [1, [2, 3]]`.\n\nReplacing a top-level element only changes that copy. Both shallow and deep copies protect the original here:\n\n```python\nshallow[0] = 99\n# original -> [1, [2, 3]]     safe\n# shallow  -> [99, [2, 3]]\n```\n\nMutating the nested list is where they differ. A shallow copy still shares that inner list:\n\n```python\nshallow[1][0] = \"X\"\n# original -> [1, [\"X\", 3]]   modified\n# shallow  -> [99, [\"X\", 3]]\n```\n\nA deep copy duplicates nested structure, so the original stays independent:\n\n```python\noriginal = [1, [2, 3]]\ndeep = copy.deepcopy(original)\ndeep[1][0] = \"Z\"\n# original -> [1, [2, 3]]     safe\n# deep     -> [1, [\"Z\", 3]]\n```\n\n**List comprehension** builds a new list in one expression.\n\n```python\nsquares = [x * x for x in range(5) if x % 2 == 0]   # [0, 4, 16]\n```\n\nPrefer a comprehension when the result is a list you will keep. Prefer a loop when you are only iterating, or when the body is several statements.\n\n| Operation | Time |\n|---|---|\n| Index / assign / `len` / `append` / `pop()` | O(1) (`append` amortized) |\n| `pop(0)`, `insert(0, x)`, `remove`, `in` | O(n) |\n| `arr + other`, `arr[:]`, comprehension of n items | O(n) |\n\n**Trap:** `a = b = []` creates one shared list. Use `a, b = [], []`.\n\n", "minutes": 12, "track": "interview", "num": 3}, {"id": "i4", "title": "Stacks and queues", "html": "<p><strong>Stack:</strong> last in, first out. A list is the usual stack: <code>append</code> to push, <code>pop()</code> to pop. Both O(1).</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>stack = []\nstack.append(1)\nstack.append(2)\ntop = stack.pop()           # 2\n# stack[-1] is peek; IndexError if empty</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Queue:</strong> first in, first out. Do <strong>not</strong> use <code>list.pop(0)</code> \u2014 that is O(n). Use <code>collections.deque</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>from collections import deque\n\nqueue = deque()\nqueue.append(&quot;a&quot;)           # enqueue at the right\nqueue.append(&quot;b&quot;)\nfront = queue.popleft()     # &#x27;a&#x27; \u2014 O(1)</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Deque</strong> is a double-ended queue. Push/pop at either end is O(1). Indexing in the middle is O(n).</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>d = deque([1, 2, 3])\nd.appendleft(0)             # deque([0, 1, 2, 3])\nd.pop()                     # 3 from the right\nd.popleft()                 # 0 from the left</code></pre>\n<p>Typical interview uses: BFS (<code>queue</code>), monotonic stack, sliding-window maximum (<code>deque</code> of indices), undo (<code>stack</code>).</p>\n<p class=\"callout\"><strong>Trap:</strong> <code>if stack:</code> is the emptiness check. <code>stack[-1]</code> on an empty list raises <code>IndexError</code>. <code>deque</code> is not hashable and cannot be sliced the way a list can.</p>", "text": "\n**Stack:** last in, first out. A list is the usual stack: `append` to push, `pop()` to pop. Both O(1).\n\n```python\nstack = []\nstack.append(1)\nstack.append(2)\ntop = stack.pop()           # 2\n# stack[-1] is peek; IndexError if empty\n```\n\n**Queue:** first in, first out. Do **not** use `list.pop(0)` \u2014 that is O(n). Use `collections.deque`.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\n\nqueue = deque()\nqueue.append(\"a\")           # enqueue at the right\nqueue.append(\"b\")\nfront = queue.popleft()     # 'a' \u2014 O(1)\n```\n\n**Deque** is a double-ended queue. Push/pop at either end is O(1). Indexing in the middle is O(n).\n\n```python\nd = deque([1, 2, 3])\nd.appendleft(0)             # deque([0, 1, 2, 3])\nd.pop()                     # 3 from the right\nd.popleft()                 # 0 from the left\n```\n\nTypical interview uses: BFS (`queue`), monotonic stack, sliding-window maximum (`deque` of indices), undo (`stack`).\n\n**Trap:** `if stack:` is the emptiness check. `stack[-1]` on an empty list raises `IndexError`. `deque` is not hashable and cannot be sliced the way a list can.\n\n", "minutes": 8, "track": "interview", "num": 4}, {"id": "i5", "title": "2-D lists", "html": "<p>A 2-D list is a list of rows. <code>grid[r][c]</code> is row first, then column.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>grid = [\n    [1, 2, 3],\n    [4, 5, 6],\n]\nrows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])\ngrid[1][0]                  # 4</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Independent rows</strong> must be created in a loop or a comprehension. <code>[row] * n</code> repeats the same row object.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code># shared rows \u2014 changing one cell changes every row\nbroken = [[0] * 3] * 2\nbroken[0][0] = 1\n# broken -&gt; [[1, 0, 0], [1, 0, 0]]\n\ngrid = [[0] * 3 for _ in range(2)]\ngrid[0][0] = 1\n# grid -&gt; [[1, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0]]</code></pre>\n<p><strong>2-D grid traversal</strong> usually walks four (or eight) directions and bounds-checks.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>dirs = [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)]\nfor r in range(rows):\n    for c in range(cols):\n        for dr, dc in dirs:\n            nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n            if 0 &lt;= nr &lt; rows and 0 &lt;= nc &lt; cols:\n                neighbor = grid[nr][nc]</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Nested list comprehension:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>pairs = [[i, j] for i in range(2) for j in range(3)]\n# [[0, 0], [0, 1], [0, 2], [1, 0], [1, 1], [1, 2]]\n\nmatrix = [[i * 3 + j for j in range(3)] for i in range(2)]\n# [[0, 1, 2], [3, 4, 5]]</code></pre>\n<p>The rightmost <code>for</code> is the inner loop. Read <code>[expr for row in grid for x in row]</code> as flatten.</p>\n<p class=\"callout\"><strong>Trap:</strong> <code>len(grid[0])</code> assumes a non-empty rectangular grid. Check <code>if not grid or not grid[0]:</code> first. Jagged rows are legal in Python and break <code>cols = len(grid[0])</code>.</p>", "text": "\nA 2-D list is a list of rows. `grid[r][c]` is row first, then column.\n\n```python\ngrid = [\n    [1, 2, 3],\n    [4, 5, 6],\n]\nrows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])\ngrid[1][0]                  # 4\n```\n\n**Independent rows** must be created in a loop or a comprehension. `[row] * n` repeats the same row object.\n\n```python\n# shared rows \u2014 changing one cell changes every row\nbroken = [[0] * 3] * 2\nbroken[0][0] = 1\n# broken -> [[1, 0, 0], [1, 0, 0]]\n\ngrid = [[0] * 3 for _ in range(2)]\ngrid[0][0] = 1\n# grid -> [[1, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0]]\n```\n\n**2-D grid traversal** usually walks four (or eight) directions and bounds-checks.\n\n```python\ndirs = [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)]\nfor r in range(rows):\n    for c in range(cols):\n        for dr, dc in dirs:\n            nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n            if 0 <= nr < rows and 0 <= nc < cols:\n                neighbor = grid[nr][nc]\n```\n\n**Nested list comprehension:**\n\n```python\npairs = [[i, j] for i in range(2) for j in range(3)]\n# [[0, 0], [0, 1], [0, 2], [1, 0], [1, 1], [1, 2]]\n\nmatrix = [[i * 3 + j for j in range(3)] for i in range(2)]\n# [[0, 1, 2], [3, 4, 5]]\n```\n\nThe rightmost `for` is the inner loop. Read `[expr for row in grid for x in row]` as flatten.\n\n**Trap:** `len(grid[0])` assumes a non-empty rectangular grid. Check `if not grid or not grid[0]:` first. Jagged rows are legal in Python and break `cols = len(grid[0])`.\n\n", "minutes": 8, "track": "interview", "num": 5}, {"id": "i6", "title": "Hash maps and hash sets", "html": "<p>A dict maps unique hashable keys to values. A set stores unique hashable elements. Average lookup/insert/delete is O(1); worst case is O(n).</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>counts = {}\ncounts[&quot;a&quot;] = 1\ncounts[&quot;a&quot;] += 1            # 2\ncounts.get(&quot;z&quot;, 0)          # 0; does not insert\n# counts[&quot;z&quot;]               # KeyError</code></pre>\n<p>**<code>Counter</code>** counts an iterable automatically. Missing keys read as <code>0</code> and are not inserted.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>from collections import Counter\n\nfreq = Counter(&quot;banana&quot;)    # Counter({&#x27;a&#x27;: 3, &#x27;n&#x27;: 2, &#x27;b&#x27;: 1})\nfreq[&quot;z&quot;]                   # 0 \u2014 not a KeyError, and not inserted\nfreq.most_common(1)         # [(&#x27;a&#x27;, 3)]\nfreq.update(&quot;aaa&quot;)\nfreq[&quot;a&quot;]                   # 6\nCounter([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3]) # Counter({3: 3, 2: 2, 1: 1})\n\ndef is_anagram(s, t):\n    return Counter(s) == Counter(t)</code></pre>\n<p>**<code>defaultdict</code>** creates and stores a default on missing-key access with <code>[]</code>. <code>d[&quot;a&quot;].append(0)</code> on a normal dict is <code>KeyError</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>from collections import defaultdict\n\npositions = defaultdict(list)\nfor i, ch in enumerate(&quot;banana&quot;):\n    positions[ch].append(i)\n# {&#x27;b&#x27;: [0], &#x27;a&#x27;: [1, 3, 5], &#x27;n&#x27;: [2, 4]}\n\nfreq = defaultdict(int)\nfor ch in &quot;banana&quot;:\n    freq[ch] += 1           # missing key starts at 0\n\ngraph = defaultdict(set)\nfor u, v in [(&quot;A&quot;, &quot;B&quot;), (&quot;A&quot;, &quot;C&quot;), (&quot;A&quot;, &quot;B&quot;)]:\n    graph[u].add(v)\n# {&#x27;A&#x27;: {&#x27;B&#x27;, &#x27;C&#x27;}}</code></pre>\n<p><code>[]</code> inserts the default; <code>.get()</code> does not: <code>defaultdict(list).get(&quot;x&quot;)</code> is <code>None</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Frequency of an iterable</td><td><code>Counter</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Count while looping</td><td><code>defaultdict(int)</code> or <code>Counter</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Group into lists</td><td><code>defaultdict(list)</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Unique neighbors / edges</td><td><code>defaultdict(set)</code></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Dict comprehension and items:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>squares = {x: x * x for x in range(4)}          # {0: 0, 1: 1, 2: 4, 3: 9}\nfor key, value in squares.items():\n    pass</code></pre>\n<p><code>keys()</code>, <code>values()</code>, and <code>items()</code> are dynamic views. Do not change dict size while iterating over it.</p>\n<p><strong>Hash set:</strong> unique elements, average O(1) membership. Order is not guaranteed. <code>{}</code> is a dict; use <code>set()</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>seen = set()\nseen.add(3)\n3 in seen                   # True\nseen.discard(9)             # no error\n# seen.remove(9)            # KeyError\nevens = {x for x in range(6) if x % 2 == 0}     # {0, 2, 4}\n\nA, B = {1, 2, 3, 4}, {3, 4, 5, 6}\nA | B                       # {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}\nA &amp; B                       # {3, 4}\nA - B                       # {1, 2}\nA ^ B                       # {1, 2, 5, 6}</code></pre>\n<p><code>set(values)</code> drops duplicates but does not keep order. To deduplicate and keep first-seen order: <code>list(dict.fromkeys(values))</code>.</p>\n<p>A <code>frozenset</code> is immutable and hashable, so it can be a dict key or a set element (a normal set cannot). <code>frozenset([&quot;A&quot;, &quot;B&quot;])</code> equals <code>frozenset([&quot;B&quot;, &quot;A&quot;])</code> \u2014 useful for undirected edges.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>len(nums) != len(set(nums))     # has a duplicate\nset(a) &amp; set(b)                 # common elements</code></pre>\n<p>Use a set for repeated membership checks. Scanning a list is O(n) each time.</p>\n<p><strong>Tuple keys</strong> work when every element is hashable. Lists cannot be keys.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>visited = set()\nvisited.add((1, 2))         # grid cell (r, c)\n# visited.add([1, 2])       # TypeError: unhashable type: &#x27;list&#x27;\npos = {(r, c): val for r, row in enumerate(grid) for c, val in enumerate(row)}</code></pre>\n<p><code>True</code> and <code>1</code> collide as dict/set keys (they compare equal and hash equal).</p>\n<p class=\"callout\"><strong>Trap:</strong> <code>d.get(key)</code> returning <code>None</code> cannot distinguish a missing key from a stored <code>None</code>. Use <code>key in d</code> when that matters.</p>", "text": "\nA dict maps unique hashable keys to values. A set stores unique hashable elements. Average lookup/insert/delete is O(1); worst case is O(n).\n\n```python\ncounts = {}\ncounts[\"a\"] = 1\ncounts[\"a\"] += 1            # 2\ncounts.get(\"z\", 0)          # 0; does not insert\n# counts[\"z\"]               # KeyError\n```\n\n**`Counter`** counts an iterable automatically. Missing keys read as `0` and are not inserted.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\nfreq = Counter(\"banana\")    # Counter({'a': 3, 'n': 2, 'b': 1})\nfreq[\"z\"]                   # 0 \u2014 not a KeyError, and not inserted\nfreq.most_common(1)         # [('a', 3)]\nfreq.update(\"aaa\")\nfreq[\"a\"]                   # 6\nCounter([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3]) # Counter({3: 3, 2: 2, 1: 1})\n\ndef is_anagram(s, t):\n    return Counter(s) == Counter(t)\n```\n\n**`defaultdict`** creates and stores a default on missing-key access with `[]`. `d[\"a\"].append(0)` on a normal dict is `KeyError`.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\npositions = defaultdict(list)\nfor i, ch in enumerate(\"banana\"):\n    positions[ch].append(i)\n# {'b': [0], 'a': [1, 3, 5], 'n': [2, 4]}\n\nfreq = defaultdict(int)\nfor ch in \"banana\":\n    freq[ch] += 1           # missing key starts at 0\n\ngraph = defaultdict(set)\nfor u, v in [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"A\", \"C\"), (\"A\", \"B\")]:\n    graph[u].add(v)\n# {'A': {'B', 'C'}}\n```\n\n`[]` inserts the default; `.get()` does not: `defaultdict(list).get(\"x\")` is `None`.\n\n| Need | Use |\n|---|---|\n| Frequency of an iterable | `Counter` |\n| Count while looping | `defaultdict(int)` or `Counter` |\n| Group into lists | `defaultdict(list)` |\n| Unique neighbors / edges | `defaultdict(set)` |\n\n**Dict comprehension and items:**\n\n```python\nsquares = {x: x * x for x in range(4)}          # {0: 0, 1: 1, 2: 4, 3: 9}\nfor key, value in squares.items():\n    pass\n```\n\n`keys()`, `values()`, and `items()` are dynamic views. Do not change dict size while iterating over it.\n\n**Hash set:** unique elements, average O(1) membership. Order is not guaranteed. `{}` is a dict; use `set()`.\n\n```python\nseen = set()\nseen.add(3)\n3 in seen                   # True\nseen.discard(9)             # no error\n# seen.remove(9)            # KeyError\nevens = {x for x in range(6) if x % 2 == 0}     # {0, 2, 4}\n\nA, B = {1, 2, 3, 4}, {3, 4, 5, 6}\nA | B                       # {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}\nA & B                       # {3, 4}\nA - B                       # {1, 2}\nA ^ B                       # {1, 2, 5, 6}\n```\n\n`set(values)` drops duplicates but does not keep order. To deduplicate and keep first-seen order: `list(dict.fromkeys(values))`.\n\nA `frozenset` is immutable and hashable, so it can be a dict key or a set element (a normal set cannot). `frozenset([\"A\", \"B\"])` equals `frozenset([\"B\", \"A\"])` \u2014 useful for undirected edges.\n\n```python\nlen(nums) != len(set(nums))     # has a duplicate\nset(a) & set(b)                 # common elements\n```\n\nUse a set for repeated membership checks. Scanning a list is O(n) each time.\n\n**Tuple keys** work when every element is hashable. Lists cannot be keys.\n\n```python\nvisited = set()\nvisited.add((1, 2))         # grid cell (r, c)\n# visited.add([1, 2])       # TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\npos = {(r, c): val for r, row in enumerate(grid) for c, val in enumerate(row)}\n```\n\n`True` and `1` collide as dict/set keys (they compare equal and hash equal).\n\n**Trap:** `d.get(key)` returning `None` cannot distinguish a missing key from a stored `None`. Use `key in d` when that matters.\n\n", "minutes": 14, "track": "interview", "num": 6}, {"id": "i7", "title": "Heaps and priority queues", "html": "<p><code>heapq</code> is a <strong>min-heap</strong> on a list. Index <code>0</code> is always the smallest item. Push and pop are O(log n). <code>heapify</code> is O(n).</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>import heapq\n\nheap = []\nheapq.heappush(heap, 5)\nheapq.heappush(heap, 1)\nheapq.heappush(heap, 3)\nsmallest = heapq.heappop(heap)   # 1; heap is now [3, 5]\npeek = heap[0]                   # smallest without removing</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Heapify</strong> rearranges an existing list in place. It does not sort the whole list.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>nums = [5, 1, 4, 2]\nheapq.heapify(nums)         # nums[0] == 1; the rest is heap-ordered, not fully sorted</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Max-heap:</strong> negate numbers, then negate again on the way out.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>max_heap = []\nfor x in [5, 1, 4]:\n    heapq.heappush(max_heap, -x)\nlargest = -heapq.heappop(max_heap)   # 5</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Custom heap:</strong> store tuples. Comparison is left to right, so put the priority first.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code># smallest deadline first; break ties by name\ntasks = []\nheapq.heappush(tasks, (2, &quot;write&quot;))\nheapq.heappush(tasks, (1, &quot;read&quot;))\nheapq.heappop(tasks)        # (1, &#x27;read&#x27;)</code></pre>\n<p>If two tuples can have equal priorities and incomparable later fields, add a unique tie-breaker (<code>count</code>) so the heap never compares the payload.</p>\n<p><strong>N smallest / n largest</strong> are convenience helpers. They do not require you to manage the heap yourself.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>heapq.nsmallest(2, [5, 1, 4, 2])    # [1, 2]\nheapq.nlargest(2, [5, 1, 4, 2])     # [5, 4]</code></pre>\n<p>Cost is O(n log k) for k results. For one pass over a stream, keep a heap of size k yourself.</p>\n<p><strong>The scenario.</strong> Find the 2 largest values in <code>[5, 1, 4, 2]</code>.</p>\n<p>A min-heap of size k keeps the current winners. The heap top is the <strong>smallest of the large values</strong>, so a new candidate replaces it only when it is bigger:</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>nums, k = [5, 1, 4, 2], 2\nheap = []\nfor x in nums:\n    heapq.heappush(heap, x)\n    if len(heap) &gt; k:\n        heapq.heappop(heap)     # drop the current smallest\n# heap holds [4, 5] (order inside the heap is not sorted)</code></pre>\n<p>Using a max-heap for \u201ck largest\u201d is the opposite idea and uses more memory (the whole array). Prefer a size-k min-heap.</p>\n<p class=\"callout\"><strong>Trap:</strong> <code>heapq</code> has no <code>heappushpop</code> requirement, but <code>heappushpop</code> and <code>heapreplace</code> exist and are faster than separate push+pop. There is no max-heap type. Do not sort after every insert \u2014 that is the point of a heap.</p>", "text": "\n`heapq` is a **min-heap** on a list. Index `0` is always the smallest item. Push and pop are O(log n). `heapify` is O(n).\n\n```python\nimport heapq\n\nheap = []\nheapq.heappush(heap, 5)\nheapq.heappush(heap, 1)\nheapq.heappush(heap, 3)\nsmallest = heapq.heappop(heap)   # 1; heap is now [3, 5]\npeek = heap[0]                   # smallest without removing\n```\n\n**Heapify** rearranges an existing list in place. It does not sort the whole list.\n\n```python\nnums = [5, 1, 4, 2]\nheapq.heapify(nums)         # nums[0] == 1; the rest is heap-ordered, not fully sorted\n```\n\n**Max-heap:** negate numbers, then negate again on the way out.\n\n```python\nmax_heap = []\nfor x in [5, 1, 4]:\n    heapq.heappush(max_heap, -x)\nlargest = -heapq.heappop(max_heap)   # 5\n```\n\n**Custom heap:** store tuples. Comparison is left to right, so put the priority first.\n\n```python\n# smallest deadline first; break ties by name\ntasks = []\nheapq.heappush(tasks, (2, \"write\"))\nheapq.heappush(tasks, (1, \"read\"))\nheapq.heappop(tasks)        # (1, 'read')\n```\n\nIf two tuples can have equal priorities and incomparable later fields, add a unique tie-breaker (`count`) so the heap never compares the payload.\n\n**N smallest / n largest** are convenience helpers. They do not require you to manage the heap yourself.\n\n```python\nheapq.nsmallest(2, [5, 1, 4, 2])    # [1, 2]\nheapq.nlargest(2, [5, 1, 4, 2])     # [5, 4]\n```\n\nCost is O(n log k) for k results. For one pass over a stream, keep a heap of size k yourself.\n\n**The scenario.** Find the 2 largest values in `[5, 1, 4, 2]`.\n\nA min-heap of size k keeps the current winners. The heap top is the **smallest of the large values**, so a new candidate replaces it only when it is bigger:\n\n```python\nnums, k = [5, 1, 4, 2], 2\nheap = []\nfor x in nums:\n    heapq.heappush(heap, x)\n    if len(heap) > k:\n        heapq.heappop(heap)     # drop the current smallest\n# heap holds [4, 5] (order inside the heap is not sorted)\n```\n\nUsing a max-heap for \u201ck largest\u201d is the opposite idea and uses more memory (the whole array). Prefer a size-k min-heap.\n\n**Trap:** `heapq` has no `heappushpop` requirement, but `heappushpop` and `heapreplace` exist and are faster than separate push+pop. There is no max-heap type. Do not sort after every insert \u2014 that is the point of a heap.\n\n", "minutes": 12, "track": "interview", "num": 7}, {"id": "i8", "title": "Sorted dicts and sorted sets", "html": "<p>Ordinary <code>dict</code> and <code>set</code> are not sorted by key. If you need ordered keys plus fast lookup, interviews usually do one of these:</p>\n<p>1. Keep a list sorted yourself and use <code>bisect</code> (stdlib, always available). 2. Use <code>sortedcontainers</code> (<code>SortedDict</code>, <code>SortedSet</code>, <code>SortedList</code>) if the environment allows a third-party package.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>import bisect\n\nvals = [1, 3, 5]\nbisect.insort(vals, 4)      # [1, 3, 4, 5] \u2014 O(n) to insert, O(log n) to find the index\ni = bisect.bisect_left(vals, 4)     # 2</code></pre>\n<p><code>bisect_left</code> is the insertion point before existing equals; <code>bisect_right</code> is after them. <code>insort</code> is insert-into-sorted-list, not a balanced tree.</p>\n<p>If <code>sortedcontainers</code> is allowed:</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>from sortedcontainers import SortedDict, SortedSet\n\nsd = SortedDict({&quot;c&quot;: 3, &quot;a&quot;: 1})\nsd[&quot;b&quot;] = 2\nlist(sd.keys())             # [&#x27;a&#x27;, &#x27;b&#x27;, &#x27;c&#x27;]\nsd.bisect_left(&quot;b&quot;)         # 1 \u2014 index of the first key &gt;= &quot;b&quot;\n\nss = SortedSet([3, 1, 2])\nss.add(2)                   # still SortedSet([1, 2, 3])\nss.bisect_left(2)           # 1</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>SortedDict</code> iterates keys in sorted order. <code>dict</code> iterates in insertion order.</li>\n<li><code>SortedSet</code> is an ordered unique collection. <code>set</code> has no positional index.</li>\n<li>Typical uses: sliding-window medians, closest-key queries, \u201cnext greater key\u201d.</li>\n<li>In a standard coding interview, say you would use a balanced tree / <code>SortedList</code>, then implement with <code>bisect</code> on a list if the library is unavailable. Insert into a Python list is O(n).</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"callout\"><strong>Trap:</strong> sorting a dict\u2019s keys on every query is O(n log n) and usually the wrong substitute for a sorted map.</p>", "text": "\nOrdinary `dict` and `set` are not sorted by key. If you need ordered keys plus fast lookup, interviews usually do one of these:\n\n1. Keep a list sorted yourself and use `bisect` (stdlib, always available).\n2. Use `sortedcontainers` (`SortedDict`, `SortedSet`, `SortedList`) if the environment allows a third-party package.\n\n```python\nimport bisect\n\nvals = [1, 3, 5]\nbisect.insort(vals, 4)      # [1, 3, 4, 5] \u2014 O(n) to insert, O(log n) to find the index\ni = bisect.bisect_left(vals, 4)     # 2\n```\n\n`bisect_left` is the insertion point before existing equals; `bisect_right` is after them. `insort` is insert-into-sorted-list, not a balanced tree.\n\nIf `sortedcontainers` is allowed:\n\n```python\nfrom sortedcontainers import SortedDict, SortedSet\n\nsd = SortedDict({\"c\": 3, \"a\": 1})\nsd[\"b\"] = 2\nlist(sd.keys())             # ['a', 'b', 'c']\nsd.bisect_left(\"b\")         # 1 \u2014 index of the first key >= \"b\"\n\nss = SortedSet([3, 1, 2])\nss.add(2)                   # still SortedSet([1, 2, 3])\nss.bisect_left(2)           # 1\n```\n\n- `SortedDict` iterates keys in sorted order. `dict` iterates in insertion order.\n- `SortedSet` is an ordered unique collection. `set` has no positional index.\n- Typical uses: sliding-window medians, closest-key queries, \u201cnext greater key\u201d.\n- In a standard coding interview, say you would use a balanced tree / `SortedList`, then implement with `bisect` on a list if the library is unavailable. Insert into a Python list is O(n).\n\n**Trap:** sorting a dict\u2019s keys on every query is O(n log n) and usually the wrong substitute for a sorted map.\n\n", "minutes": 8, "track": "interview", "num": 8}, {"id": "i9", "title": "Costs to remember", "html": "<p>Assume n elements and ordinary fixed-size values. Hash-table costs are average; individual operations can be O(n) in the worst case.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Structure / operation</th><th>Time</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>List index, <code>append</code>, <code>pop()</code></td><td>O(1)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>List insert/pop at front, scan, <code>in</code></td><td>O(n)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>sort</code> / <code>sorted</code></td><td>O(n log n)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dict / set lookup, insert, delete</td><td>O(1) average</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>deque</code> append/pop at either end</td><td>O(1)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>heappush</code> / <code>heappop</code></td><td>O(log n)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>heapify</code></td><td>O(n)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>nsmallest</code> / <code>nlargest</code> of k</td><td>O(n log k)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>bisect</code> search</td><td>O(log n)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>bisect.insort</code></td><td>O(n)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>", "text": "\nAssume n elements and ordinary fixed-size values. Hash-table costs are average; individual operations can be O(n) in the worst case.\n\n| Structure / operation | Time |\n|---|---|\n| List index, `append`, `pop()` | O(1) |\n| List insert/pop at front, scan, `in` | O(n) |\n| `sort` / `sorted` | O(n log n) |\n| Dict / set lookup, insert, delete | O(1) average |\n| `deque` append/pop at either end | O(1) |\n| `heappush` / `heappop` | O(log n) |\n| `heapify` | O(n) |\n| `nsmallest` / `nlargest` of k | O(n log k) |\n| `bisect` search | O(log n) |\n| `bisect.insort` | O(n) |\n\n", "minutes": 5, "track": "interview", "num": 9}, {"id": "i10", "title": "Ready to move on", "html": "<p>You should be able to explain these without looking them up:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>sort</code> vs <code>sorted</code>, <code>key=</code>, stability, and why <code>arr = arr.sort()</code> is wrong.</li>\n<li>Unpacking, <code>enumerate</code>, <code>zip</code>, and chained comparisons.</li>\n<li>Why <code>pop(0)</code> is the wrong queue, and when to use <code>deque</code>.</li>\n<li>Independent 2-D rows vs <code>[row] * n</code>, and 4-direction bounds checks.</li>\n<li>Shallow vs deep copy on <code>original = [1, [2, 3]]</code>.</li>\n<li><code>Counter</code> vs <code>defaultdict</code> vs <code>dict</code> (<code>[]</code> inserts on defaultdict, returns 0 on Counter), tuple keys, <code>set</code> vs <code>frozenset</code>, and <code>list(dict.fromkeys(...))</code>.</li>\n<li>Min-heap vs negated max-heap, <code>heapify</code>, and a size-k heap for top-k.</li>\n<li><code>bisect</code> as the stdlib stand-in for a sorted map/set.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Next: pick problems that force these tools \u2014 arrays/hashing, two pointers, sliding windows, stack, BFS with <code>deque</code>, and top-k with <code>heapq</code>.</p>", "text": "\nYou should be able to explain these without looking them up:\n\n- `sort` vs `sorted`, `key=`, stability, and why `arr = arr.sort()` is wrong.\n- Unpacking, `enumerate`, `zip`, and chained comparisons.\n- Why `pop(0)` is the wrong queue, and when to use `deque`.\n- Independent 2-D rows vs `[row] * n`, and 4-direction bounds checks.\n- Shallow vs deep copy on `original = [1, [2, 3]]`.\n- `Counter` vs `defaultdict` vs `dict` (`[]` inserts on defaultdict, returns 0 on Counter), tuple keys, `set` vs `frozenset`, and `list(dict.fromkeys(...))`.\n- Min-heap vs negated max-heap, `heapify`, and a size-k heap for top-k.\n- `bisect` as the stdlib stand-in for a sorted map/set.\n\nNext: pick problems that force these tools \u2014 arrays/hashing, two pointers, sliding windows, stack, BFS with `deque`, and top-k with `heapq`.\n", "minutes": 3, "track": "interview", "num": 10}];
const SCHEDULE = "<h3>Interview prep schedule</h3>\n<p>Working assumption: 60\u201375 minutes a day, five focused days per week, one light review day, and one rest/buffer day. Use relative weeks until an interview date is confirmed. This plan focuses on Python and general coding interviews; ML theory, ML system design, and role-specific rounds need a separate track once the format is known.</p>\n<h3>Week 1 \u2014 Python revision now</h3>\n<p>Use Python revision notes, based on your screenshot topics.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>Content</th><th>Time</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>Variables, references, types, casting; arithmetic and Boolean operators</td><td>45\u201360 min</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2</td><td>Functions, return values, scope, defaults; conditionals and truthiness</td><td>60 min</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3</td><td>Loops; strings, indexing, slicing, immutability</td><td>60 min</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4</td><td>Lists, tuples, aliasing, shallow copies, sorting</td><td>60\u201375 min</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5</td><td>Sets, dictionaries, hashability, operation costs</td><td>60\u201375 min</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6</td><td>Stdin and exceptions; reread marked weak spots</td><td>30\u201345 min</td></tr>\n<tr><td>7</td><td>Rest / buffer</td><td>\u2014</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>For each revision session: 5 minutes recalling yesterday&#x27;s concepts, 30\u201340 minutes reading and tracing examples, then 10\u201315 minutes explaining the key rules aloud and recording weak spots. Optional: type a worked example from memory, then compare it. No problem sets or timed questions in this first phase.</p>\n<p>Move on when you can explain the notes&#x27; readiness checklist without prompting. If needed, repeat only the weak sections rather than restarting the whole week.</p>\n<p>After that checklist, revise <strong>Python for coding interviews</strong> \u2014 the standard-library tools used in solutions: sorting, pythonic iteration, lists and 2-D grids, <code>deque</code>, hash maps/sets, <code>heapq</code>, and <code>bisect</code>. Then begin Week 2 problem patterns.</p>\n<h3>Weeks 2\u20136 \u2014 DSA progression</h3>\n<p>The following is the roadmap; detailed notes and question selection come after the Python revision. Within each topic, revise the concept and implementation first, then introduce practice.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Week</th><th>Fundamentals to revise</th><th>Practice phase, when ready</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>2</td><td>Interview Python tools, then Big-O, arrays/strings, hash maps/sets, sorting, prefix sums</td><td>Arrays/hashing, two pointers, sliding windows</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3</td><td>Stack, queue/<code>deque</code>, linked lists, binary search and boundaries</td><td>Stack problems, linked-list operations, binary search</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4</td><td>Recursion and call stack; tree traversals; BSTs; heaps/priority queues</td><td>DFS/BFS on trees, BST operations, top-k</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5</td><td>Graph representations; visited sets; BFS/DFS; backtracking; topological sorting</td><td>Grid/graph traversal, subsets/permutations, dependencies</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6</td><td>Intervals and greedy reasoning; DP states/transitions/base cases; basic bit operations</td><td>Introductory 1D/2D DP, mixed review, mock interviews</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>For a five-day DSA week: days 1\u20132 cover concepts and worked implementations, days 3\u20134 cover selected problems, day 5 revisits mistakes and mixes earlier topics. Day 6 is light recall; day 7 is a buffer. Week 6 should reserve two sessions for mocks. Treat this as an initial pass, not a guarantee of mastery; extend weak topics as needed.</p>\n<p>A later practice session can use: 10 minutes recall, 15 minutes concept review, 30\u201340 minutes independent coding, 10 minutes explanation and mistake review. Start with one carefully reviewed problem per session rather than chasing a problem count.</p>\n<h3>Rebuild independent fluency</h3>\n<p>During recall and later practice, close coding agents and autocomplete. Use assistance after your own attempt to review reasoning and identify gaps. Revisit each weak concept the next day, about three days later, and the following week.</p>\n<p>For each future problem, explain the approach before coding, state the invariant and complexity, then trace empty/single-element inputs, duplicates, and boundary cases. Record the specific mistake and the rule that prevents it.</p>\n<h3>If the interview becomes urgent</h3>\n<p>For roughly two weeks of lead time: use 2\u20133 days for Python, then prioritize Big-O, arrays/hashing, two pointers/sliding windows, binary search, stacks/queues, and tree/graph traversal. Reserve the final two sessions for mocks and reviewing mistakes. Add heaps, backtracking, and DP according to the interview format and remaining time.</p>\n<p>Start today with Week 1, Day 1. The immediate deliverable is confident recall of Python behavior; question practice begins after that revision.</p>";
